2. Meaning of supply
Section 9-10 of the GST Act defines what is meant by a supply.
Subsection 9-10(1) provides that a supply is any form of supply whatsoever.
Subsection 9-10(2) provides examples of things that are supplies:
•	supplying goods
•	supplying services
•	providing advice or information
•	granting, assigning or surrendering real property
•	creating, granting, transferring, assigning or surrendering any right
•	making a financial supply
•	entering into, or releasing from, an obligation:
-	to do anything
-	to refrain from an act
-	to tolerate an act or situation
A supply includes any combination of any 2 or more of those supplies.
Subsection 9-10(3) clarifies that it does not matter whether the supply is lawful.
 Example 2: supplies
A supply of goods
Shoes-r-us sells a pair of shoes to a customer.
This is a supply of goods.
Entering into an obligation
The owner of a restaurant sells their business. As part of the deal, they agree not to open a similar restaurant in the neighbourhood within the following 24 months.
This is a supply of an agreement to refrain from an act.
 
 Reflection question 3: use of the terms ‘any form’, ‘without limiting’ and ‘includes’
In defining the meaning of supply, section 9-10 of the GST Act uses the terms ‘any form’, ‘without limiting’ and ‘includes’.
What do you think the implications of these terms are?
Answer
They imply that the meaning of supply is very broad.
Paragraph 33 of Goods and Services Tax Ruling GSTR 2006/9 Goods and services tax: supplies explains that ‘a supply is any form of supply whatsoever' in subsection 9-10(1) and covers all supplies regardless of whether they concern goods or services.
The explanatory memorandum to the GST Act confirms this interpretation by stating:
‘This is defined broadly and is intended to encompass supplies as widely as possible’ (in relation to subsection 9-10(1)).’
‘[It] provides a list of things that are included as supplies. It is not an exhaustive list. It does not limit the possible breadth of subsection 9-10(1)’ (in relation to subsection 9-10(2)).’
2.1 Supplies of money and digital currency
Subsection 9-10(4) of the GST Act excludes 2 things from the meaning of supply:
•	A supply of money unless the money is provided as consideration for a supply that is a supply of money or digital currency.
•	A supply of digital currency unless the digital currency is provided as consideration for a supply that is a supply of digital currency or money.
 More information
Money and digital currency are defined in section 195-1 of the GST Act.
 
 Example 3: money
Money is not a supply
Kaiya buys office equipment from a retailer. Kaiya’s payment to the retailer is not a supply.
Money is a supply
Foreign Funds Bank sells US currency to Luc, who pays the equivalent amount in Australian currency as consideration. Foreign Funds Bank’s supply of the US currency is a supply.
2.2 Characteristics of a supply
GSTR 2006/9 explains our view of supplies including the:
•	concept of supply in the GST Act
•	meaning of a supply in section 9-10
•	characteristics of a supply (referred to as ‘propositions’).
Paragraph 21 of GSTR 2006/9 explains the propositions for characterising supplies and analysing more complex transactions flow from the concept of supply in the context of both a broad-based GST and the text and structure of the GST Act. The propositions are not universal as they may have exceptions or be qualified by the operation of particular provisions of the GST Act.
There are 16 propositions in all. The first 10 cover ‘supply in the context of a transaction’. The remainder cover ‘supply in the context of a tripartite arrangement. The 2 sets of propositions are listed in paragraphs 22 and 23 respectively:
•	Proposition 1 - For every supply there is a supplier.
•	Proposition 2 - Generally, for every supply there is a recipient and an acquisition.
•	Proposition 3 - A supply may be mixed, composite or neither.
•	Proposition 4 - A transaction may involve 2 or more supplies.
•	Proposition 5 - An entity will make a supply if it provides something to another entity.
•	Proposition 6 - ‘Supply’ usually, but not necessarily, requires something to be passed from one entity to another.
•	Proposition 7 - An entity cannot make a supply to itself.
•	Proposition 8 - A supply cannot be made by more than one entity.
•	Proposition 9 - Creation of expectations alone does not establish a supply.
•	Proposition 10 - It is necessary to analyse the transaction that occurs, not the transaction that might have occurred.
•	Proposition 11 - The agreement is the logical starting point when working out the entity making the supply and the recipient of that supply.
•	Proposition 12 - Transactions that are neither based in an agreement that binds the parties in some way nor involve a supply of goods, services, or some other thing, do not establish a supply.
•	Proposition 13 - When A has an agreement with B for B to provide a supply to C, there is a supply made by B to A (contractual flow) that B provides to C (actual flow).
•	Proposition 14 - A third party may pay for a supply but not be the recipient of the supply.
•	Proposition 15 - One set of activities may constitute the making of 2 (or more) supplies.
•	Proposition 16 - The total fact situation will determine the nature of a transaction, the entity that makes a supply and the recipient of the supply.
