5. Indirect tax zone
Paragraph 9-5(c) of the GST Act provides that for a supply to be taxable it must be connected with the indirect tax zone.
5.1 Meaning of ‘indirect tax zone’
 Attention
The GST Act initially used the term ‘Australia’ rather than ‘indirect tax zone’. You will find that many of our publications (in particular our website) still use the term ‘Australia’.
‘Indirect tax zone’ is defined in section 195-1 as Australia (within the meaning of the ITAA 1997), but does not include any of the following:
•	the external territories
•	an offshore area for the purpose of the Offshore Petroleum and Greenhouse Gas Storage Act 2006 other than an installation (within the meaning of the Customs Act) that is deemed by section 5C of the Customs Act 1901 to be part of Australia and that is located in an offshore area.
The indirect tax zone is effectively geographical Australia but without the specific exclusions. It includes the coastal sea and the continental shelf.
5.2 Meaning of ‘connected with the indirect tax zone’
Section 9-25 describes when a supply is connected with the indirect tax zone.
The rules are divided into 3 types of supplies:
•	supplies of goods
•	supplies of real property
•	supplies of anything else.
 
5.3 Supplies of goods
Section 195-1 defines ‘goods’ as any form of tangible personal property.
Paragraphs 6 and 7 of Goods and Services Tax Ruling GSTR 2018/2 Goods and services tax: supplies of goods connected with the indirect tax zone (Australia) explain our view on a supply of goods.
A supply of goods is a supply of any form of tangible personal property. That is, any form of personal property that has a physical existence. Therefore, it does not include:
•	intangible personal property such as intellectual property like a copyright
•	land or an interest in land.
A supply of goods is not limited to a sale of goods. It can include other means of supply such as a lease or hire of the goods.
The rules for goods are divided into 3 categories:
•	supplies of goods wholly within the indirect tax zone
•	supplies of goods from the indirect zone (exports)
•	supplies of goods to the indirect tax zone (imports).
Supplies of goods wholly within the indirect tax zone
Subsection 9-25(1) of the GST Act provides that a supply of goods is connected with the indirect tax zone if the goods are delivered (or made available) to the recipient in the indirect tax zone.
Paragraphs 10 to 21 of GSTR 2018/2 explain our view on when such supplies are connected with the indirect tax zone.
Circumstances in which goods are delivered by a supplier to the recipient in the indirect tax zone include:
•	where the supplier physically delivers the goods from a place in the indirect tax zone to the recipient's nominated place in the indirect tax zone
•	where the supplier has the goods imported into the indirect tax zone, shipped themselves, and then delivered to the recipient in the indirect tax zone.
'Made available' refers to the situation where goods are not delivered to the recipient but the supplier makes the goods physically available them in the indirect tax zone. For example, a supplier may make goods available for collection.
 
 Example 7: goods delivered in the indirect tax zone
Enkai, a computer wholesaler in Perth, sells computers to JP Pty Ltd, a computer retailer in Adelaide. The computers are transported from Enkai's warehouse in Perth and delivered to the premises of JP Pty Ltd in Adelaide.
This supply of goods is connected with the indirect tax zone as the computers (goods) are delivered to JP Pty Ltd, the recipient, in the indirect tax zone.
Supplies of goods from the indirect zone (exports)
Subsection 9-25(2) of the GST Act provides that a supply of goods is connected with the indirect tax zone if the goods are removed (exported) from the indirect tax zone as part of the supply.
Paragraphs 22 to 29 of GSTR 2018/2 explain our view on when such supplies are connected with the indirect tax zone.
'Removed' has its ordinary meaning. 'Remove' means to move from a place, to move or shift to another place, or to displace from a position.
 Attention
Although a supply of exported goods is connected with the indirect tax zone, the supply will generally be GST-free under section 38-185 of the GST Act.
 Example 8: goods removed from the indirect tax zone
BrisCo Pty Ltd sells souvenirs to overseas retailers.
The souvenirs are to be shipped to their overseas purchasers from BrisCo Pty Ltd's premises in Australia. The souvenirs are removed from Australia by export as part of the supply.
The supply is connected with Australia.
Supplies of goods to the indirect zone (imports)
Subsection 9-25(3) of the GST Act provides that a supply of goods is connected with the indirect tax zone if:
•	the goods are brought into the indirect tax zone
•	the supplier imports the goods.
Paragraphs 30 to 37 of GSTR 2018/2 explain our view on when such supplies are connected with the indirect tax zone.
A supplier imports the goods into Australia where it is the importer for the purposes of determining who (if any entity) makes a creditable importation.
 Attention
Subsection 9-25(3) of the GST Act does not apply to a supply of goods that involves goods imported by the recipient.
5.4 Supplies of real property
Section 195-1 defines real property to include:
•	any interest in or right over land
•	a personal right to call for or be granted any interest in or right over land
•	a licence to occupy land or any other contractual right exercisable over or in relation to land.
Paragraph 6 of GSTR 2018/1 explains land means physical land.
Subsection 9-25(4) of the GST Act provides that a supply of real property is connected with the indirect zone if the real property (or the land to which it relates) is in the indirect tax zone.
Goods and Services Tax Ruling GSTR 2018/1 Goods and services tax: supplies of real property connected with the indirect tax zone (Australia) explains our view on when a supply of real property is connected with the indirect tax zone.
Paragraph 9 of GSTR 2018/1 lists the following as some examples of when a supply of real property is connected with the indirect tax zone:
•	the sale of land situated in the indirect tax zone·
•	the grant, assignment or surrender of a lease or licence of land situated in the indirect tax zone
•	a personal right to call for or be granted any interest or right over land in the indirect tax zone
•	the grant of a put or call option over land situated in the indirect tax zone
•	a licence to occupy land in the indirect tax zone
•	the grant of contractual rights to occupy or stay at accommodation in the indirect tax zone. This would include a stay at a hotel or motel on presentation of a voucher or travel document.
 
 Example 9: supply of real property
Jamal, a resident of Melbourne, sells a commercial property that they own in Perth to Franco, a resident of the United States.
The commercial property is land located in the indirect tax zone. The supply is connected with the indirect tax zone because the land is in the indirect tax zone.
5.5 Supplies of anything else
Subsection 9-25(5) of the GST Act provides that a supply of anything other than goods or real property is connected with the indirect tax zone if any of the following apply:
•	the thing is done in the indirect tax zone
•	the supplier makes the supply through an enterprise they carry on in the indirect tax zone
•	all of the following apply
-	neither of the first 2 items apply
-	the supply is a right or option to acquire another thing
-	the supply of that other thing would be connected with the indirect tax zone
•	the recipient of the supply is an Australian consumer.
Thing is done in the indirect tax zone
Paragraph 9-25(5)(a) of the GST Act provides that the supply is connected with the indirect tax zone if the thing is done in the indirect tax zone.
Paragraphs 29 to 60 of Goods and Services Tax Ruling GSTR 2019/1 Goods and services tax: supply of anything other than goods or real property connected with the indirect tax zone (Australia) explain our view on when something is done in the indirect tax zone.
If there is a need to determine whether paragraph 9-25(5)(a) of the GST Act is satisfied, then consider:
•	what the 'thing' being supplied is
•	where that 'thing' is 'done'.
 
 Attention
it is not necessary to determine if paragraph 9-25(5)(a) applies if other provisions are easier to satisfy.
The thing being supplied
It is the 'thing' supplied which must be characterised for GST purposes. 'Thing' includes but is not limited to a service, advice, information, a right or a digital product.
The types of supplies that are covered by subsection 9-25(5) include:
•	a supply of a service
•	subcontracted services
•	providing advice or information
•	creating, granting, transferring, assigning or surrendering a right
•	entering into, or releasing from, an obligation.
Where the thing is done
The term 'done' is not defined in the GST Act and takes its ordinary meaning. Where a thing is 'done' depends on the nature of the thing being supplied. Depending on what is supplied, 'done' can mean various things including performed, executed, completed or finished.
 Example 10: supply of services
Service done in the indirect tax zone
Account Pty Ltd, an accounting firm with offices in Melbourne, supplies accounting and taxation services.
The supply of any accounting and taxation services in Melbourne (or elsewhere in the indirect tax zone) is done in the indirect tax zone.
Services not done in the indirect tax zone
An employee of Account Pty Ltd flies to London and performs taxation services in London.
The supply of those services is not done in the indirect tax zone. No services are performed in the indirect tax zone. All of the services are performed in the UK.
Through an enterprise carried on in the indirect tax zone
Paragraph 9-25(b) of the GST Act provides that the supply is connected with the indirect tax zone if the supplier makes the supply through an enterprise they carry on in the indirect tax zone.
Subsection 9-27(1) provides that an entity carries on an enterprise in the indirect tax zone if one or more relevant individuals carry on the enterprise in the indirect tax zone, and any of the following apply:
•	the enterprise is carried on through a fixed place in the indirect tax zone
•	the enterprise has been carried on through one or more places in the indirect tax zone for more than 183 days in a 12-month period
•	the entity intends to carry on the enterprise through one or more places in the indirect tax zone for more than 183 days in a 12-month period.
Paragraphs 8 to 28 of GSTR 2019/1 explain our view on when a supplier makes a supply through an enterprise they carry on in the indirect tax zone.
 Example 11: supply made through an enterprise carried on in the indirect tax zone
Oz Research Pty Ltd, an Australian company carries on an enterprise in the indirect tax zone. It has no employees based outside Australia. Oz Research Pty Ltd has patents registered globally.
The managing director attends a trade show in New Zealand. They enter into a contract with an Australian customer to grant a licence to exploit one of its patents.
Although the contract was entered into in New Zealand, the supply is connected with the enterprises that Oz Research Pty Ltd carries on in the indirect tax zone. Therefore, the supply is made through that enterprise.
Right or option
Paragraph 9-25(5)(c) of the GST Act provides that the supply is connected with the indirect tax zone if the supply is a supply of a right or option to acquire something that would be connected with the indirect tax zone.
 
 Attention
Paragraph 9-25(5)(c) does not apply if either paragraph 9-25(5)(a) or (b) apply.
Paragraphs 61 to 65 of GSTR 2019/1 explain our view on when the supply of such a right is connected with the indirect tax zone.
Recipient is an Australian consumer
Subsection 9-25(7) defines an Australian consumer as:
•	an Australian resident other than those who are solely residents because they are residents of an external Territory, and either:
-	are not registered for GST, or
-	are registered but did not acquire the thing solely or partly for their enterprise.
Paragraph 9-25(5)(d) provides that the supply is connected with the indirect tax zone if the recipient of the supply is an Australian consumer. Paragraph 9-25(5)(d) ensures that such supplies receive similar GST treatment whether they are supplied by a local or foreign supplier.
Goods and Services Tax Ruling GSTR 2017/1 Goods and services tax: making cross-border supplies to Australian consumers explains our view on such supplies.
The types of supplies covered include:
•	supplies of digital products, such as streaming or downloading of movies, music, apps, games, e-books
•	services such as consultancy and professional services.
5.6 Goods installed or assembled in the indirect tax zone
Sometimes a supply will involve a supply of goods that are brought into, and installed or assembled in, the indirect tax zone.
Subsection 9-25(6) of the GST Act provides that in such a case, the supply is treated as if it were 2 supplies:
•	a supply of the installation or assembly service
•	a supply of the goods.
5.7 Excluded supplies by non-residents
Section 9-26 of the GST Act excludes certain supplies by non-residents from being connected with the indirect tax zone.
Section 9-26 provides that a supply is not connected with the indirect tax zone if:
•	the supplier is a non-resident
•	the supplier does not make the supply through an enterprise that they carry on in the indirect tax zone
•	the supply is covered by the following table.
Item	The supply	How the supply is made
1	Inbound intangible supplies	A supply of anything other than goods or real property if:
•	the thing is done in the indirect tax zone, and
•	the recipient is an Australian-based business recipient of the supply.
2	Intangible supplies between non-residents	A supply of anything other than goods or real property if:
•	the thing is done in the indirect tax zone, and
•	the recipient is a non-resident that acquires the thing supplied solely for the purpose of an enterprise that the recipient carries on outside the indirect tax zone.
3	Supply between non-residents of leased goods	A supply by way of transfer of ownership of leased goods if:
•	the recipient is a non-resident that does not acquire the thing supplied solely or partly for the purpose of an enterprise that the recipient carries on in the indirect tax zone, and
•	the lessee
-	made a taxable importation of the goods before the supply was made, and
-	continues to lease the goods on substantially similar terms and conditions after the supply is made.
4	Supply by way of continued lease of goods from item 3	a supply made by way of lease if:
•	the recipient is the lessee referred to in paragraph (b) of item 3 of this table, and
•	the lease is the lease referred to in subparagraph (ii) of that paragraph.
