1. Business or Hobby

The question of whether an activity is a business or a hobby is extremely important for tax purposes.

If we are to educate clients and carry out compliance activities effectively, it is essential we can distinguish between business and hobby activities.

A taxpayer is making the following inquiry from you.

“Hi, my name is Marie. I work as a stenographer, but I started making and selling chocolates to workmates last year. It's really taken off – people love my chocolate! I've been selling a couple of kilos a week over the past few months and I just completed an advanced chocolate making course. Can I claim the cost of the course as a tax deduction?”
Fact or Law

Whether an activity amounts to carrying on a business or hobby can be a grey area. It is a question of fact, not law – with each set of facts judged on its own merits. The relevant facts are weighed in combination in order to arrive at a subjective decision.
Impact

The impact of business status for tax purposes is much clearer. If an activity is merely a hobby or pastime, receipts will not be assessed as income and expenses will not be allowed as deductions.
Business status

If a taxpayer is carrying on a business, income from that business is generally assessable and expenses are generally allowable deductions under the Income Tax Assessment Act 1997 (ITAA 1997)  or the Income Tax Assessment Act 1936 (ITAA 1936) ).
Consequences of business status

Numerous other consequences may also apply, including:

    trading stock provisions, for example, under Division 70 of the ITAA 1997 
    special concessions where a business of primary production is conducted - Division 385 of the ITAA 1997
    non-commercial loss provisions contained in Division 35 of the ITAA 1997 .
    certain capital business related costs deductible over five years under section 40-880 of the ITAA 1997 .
    from 1 July 2007, taxpayers may satisfy the definition of a ‘small business entity’ and be entitled to a range of concessions under Subdivision 328-C of the ITAA 1997 (subject to any additional criteria in respect to each concession) .
    the entity carrying on the business may register for GST, as set out in Division 23 of the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999 (the GST Act) .
    the entity must comply with the requirements to keep business records that document and explain all transactions and acts as set out, for example, in section 262A of the ITAA 1936 .

Definitions
Business

The term ‘business’ is defined in the ITAA 1997 as: ‘includes any profession, trade, employment, vocation or calling, but does not include occupation as an employee.’
Inclusions

The use of the word ‘includes’ broadens the ordinary meaning of the word business. This definition encompasses not only activities falling under the natural meaning of the term, but also specifically includes any profession, trade, employment, vocation or calling.
Exclusions

Note that the definition of ‘business’ specifically excludes an ‘occupation as an employee’.
Exercise

Now that you understand the definition - what do you think about Marie's chocolate making activity?

Is it likely to be a business or a hobby?
Question

What do you think? Select which answer you most agree with from the options below.

    Yes, this sounds like a weekly vocation, so it must be a business.
    No, as an employee she is specifically excluded.
    I'm undecided, just tell me more about the chocolate!

Responses

    If you chose one: She sells chocolate each week, but is this fact alone enough to qualify as a business?
    If you chose two: She is employed, but what about her other activities?
    If you chose three: The legal definition is broad and Marie has not really given us much information. Exactly what facts should you look for?

Common Law

A legal definition cannot cover every activity that a taxpayer may classify as a business. We look to the common law for clarification.
Fact and degree

Werle & Co v Colquhoun (1888) 20 QBD 753, established that whether an activity amounts to the carrying on of a business is a question of fact and degree, not law. This view was further emphasised in Evans v Federal Commissioner of Taxation (1989) 20 ATR 922; 89 ATC 4540 .

Hill J noted at ATR 939; ATC 4554 .

'The question of whether a particular activity constitutes a business is often a difficult one involving as it does questions of fact and degree - each of the cases depends upon its own facts and in the ultimate is unhelpful in the resolution of some other and different fact situation.'
Nature and Extent

Martin v Federal Commissioner of Taxation (1953) 90 CLR 477 (Martin Case) established that the determination is made by considering the nature and extent of the activities undertaken, together with the individual’s purpose.

Ultimately, the decision is based on the large or general impression given by the activity under review.
Business Indicators

Over the years, numerous cases have considered whether certain activities amount to the carrying on of a business. From these cases, various business indicators have evolved.

Ferguson v Federal Commissioner of Taxation (1979) 9 ATR 873; 79 ATC 4261 (Ferguson Case ), provides a full discussion of some of the main business indicators.
Taxation Ruling

Taxation Ruling TR 97/11: Income tax: am I carrying on a business of primary production?   sets out the Commissioner's opinion on how to determine whether an activity amounts to the carrying on of a business of primary production.

These indicators are not specific to the primary production industry. They can be used to determine whether any activity amounts to the carrying on of a business more generally. Print a copy of the indicators for your reference.

