Case 1
The facts

A taxpayer was an employee of a business located in North Queensland but he usually spent his annual holidays in Victoria.

While in Victoria one year, he purchased a female Angora goat for $3,000. He paid a further $2,000 in advance for stud services for the goat. He also incurred expenses for insurance, interest, stamp duty and bank charges, as well as subscriptions to relevant journals and reference books.

The goat grazed on a stud farm. All decisions relating to the goat were taken by the stud operators. The taxpayer's interests were cared for by a friend who was a veterinary surgeon.

The taxpayer expected that over six years he would make a $6,900 profit from sales of the goat's offspring, but the goat died after producing only two offspring. Of these two goats, one died and the other was used for breeding purposes.

The remaining animal produced only two offspring before the taxpayer sold his animals at a loss; bringing the venture to a close three years after it began.

The income received by the taxpayer from this venture consisted of proceeds of the eventual sale of the animals, a refund of the service fee and insurance recovery.

The taxpayer claimed deductions for outgoings incurred during the three income years on the basis that he was carrying on a business of primary production.
Weigh the indicators

Answer Yes or No to the following:

    Significant commercial purpose or character?
    More than intention to engage in business?
    Purpose and prospect of profit?
    Repetition and regularity?
    Similar manner to others in the industry?
    Planned, organised and businesslike?
    Better describe as a business rather than a hobby, sport or recreation?
    Overall, is this a business?

The result
This is a business

Significant commercial character

These are the facts from Federal Commissioner of Taxation v J R Walker (1985) (Walker Case) 16 ATR 331; 85 ATC 4179. (to access this reference click here)

In the Walker Case it was found that the taxpayer's Angora goat breeding activity was conducted on a commercial basis, despite the small size of the operation.

The factors to note are:

    a profit making purpose existed
    consultations with an expert occurred
    there was repetition and regularity
    the activity was conducted in a businesslike manner.

Case 2
The facts

A taxpayer earns most of his income from practising as a city barrister, but his parents still live in the country town he was born in.

For several years the taxpayer had an interest, either through a joint venture or partnerships, in grazing sheep. The sheep grazed on land obtained for him by his father or one of his three brothers, who also looked after the animals. After the sheep were sold, the taxpayer then acquired two cows and agisted them for a number of years.

The cows were held on a common where his father retained certain rights. The pound keeper reported any unusual features about the cattle to the taxpayer's father or his brothers. The maximum number of head entitled to be on the common was three cows and calves (but this was overlooked in good seasons).

The average number of stock on hand at the end of each year was four or five, whilst the number of head sold was two or three each year. The average natural increase was between two and three.

In the year in question, there was a gross profit of $153 and after payment of agisting fees of $84. A net profit of $69 remained.
