This is NOT a business

These are some of the facts in McInnes v Federal Commissioner of Taxation (1977) 7 ATR 373; 77 ATC 4167 (McInnes Case). (to access this reference click here)

The taxpayer's activities did not amount to the carrying on of a business of primary production.

It was held that the taxpayer's activities lacked significant commercial purpose, because of:

    the small amount of stock and sales made
    the small profit earned
    the fact that the activities had little in common with those that would normally be associated with a grazing business.

Not for profit?

Although profit-making purpose is important, there are limited situations where activities that are not conducted for profit are still considered to be carrying on a business. For example, charitable organisations may be carrying on a business, even though they have no profit-making intention.

While the purpose to profit is generally an important consideration, it does not necessarily follow that an activity that a taxpayer intends to profit from will actually be profitable.

Legal opinion is …in determining whether a business is being carried on it is, in my view, proper to consider as one of the elements, whether the activities under consideration could ever result in a profit….

In the ATR 876; ATC 4264 Ferguson Case (to access this reference click here) Chief Justice Bowen and Justice Franki considered when profit making can be expected to be evident in the life of a business. Bowen CJ and Franki J, Ferguson noted:

"The nature of activities particularly whether they have the purpose of profit may be important. However an immediate purpose of profit-making, in a particular income year does not appear to be essential. Certainly it may be held a person is carrying on business notwithstanding his profit is small or even where he is making a loss."
