Case 4
The facts

A barrister purchased 7½ acres near Brisbane for the purpose of building a home. He realised the land was fertile and decided that he would also plant trees.

He obtained some advice before planting 1,800 pine trees. In time, however, many of these trees were overgrown by wattle and some were destroyed by a bushfire. The value of the pine trees remaining was fairly trivial. A return of less than $100 a year on average was expected to occur.

He obtained advice that suggested there would be a market for macadamia nut trees and avocado pear trees, where demand would exceed the supply. He knew that there would be no return from this activity for seven years, but after this timeframe there would be around $3,000 return per year.

Consequently, he planted 75 macadamia nut trees and 30 avocado pear trees.

Frost destroyed 12 of the avocado pear trees, a bushfire destroyed 50 of the macadamia nut trees and the rest were damaged (but were restored). Most of the avocado pear trees were destroyed or damaged by the same bushfire so that only two remained in good condition. Later on, 35 new macadamia nut trees were planted.

The taxpayer admitted he should have watered his trees more than he did. He did not set up a fire break. The irrigation system and his treatment of grasses and regrowth were poor.

No one else in the area was commercially growing macadamia nut trees or avocado pear trees.

The taxpayer did not obtain any income from this activity in the year in question, or in any prior years.
