Purpose and prospect of profit.

These are some of the facts from Tweddle v Federal Commissioner of Taxation (1942) 180 CLR 1. The taxpayer wanted to claim deductions from the farming properties. The Commissioner argued:

    the purchase of the properties was the satisfaction of a childhood dream rather than an activity undertaken with business intent
    the properties had never returned a profit, and in all likelihood would not do so.

The court accepted the taxpayer’s view and set some guidance on application of the Income Tax Assessment Act 1997 to some businesses. If the activity is carried on in a businesslike manner, even at a loss, it is more likely a business.

The courts, and the ATO, cannot close down a business or deny deductions because it runs at a loss. If the activity were to return profit, would that profit be taxable? If so, it is likely that in the event of a loss, it would be deductible.

"The Commissioner is not entitled to say he (the taxpayer) must close down his business because it is unprofitable and cut his losses, even if it might be better in his own interests and although it would certainly be better in the interests of the Commissioner if he did so"

" …if the appellant succeeds and makes a profit, it will plainly be taxable, and it is difficult to see how his activities could at that moment of time be transmogrified from an indulgence in a somewhat unusual form of recreation into the carrying on of a business…" Williams J
