Organised and businesslike

Generally, if an activity is undertaken in an organised, systematic, businesslike manner it is more likely to be a business.

In the Ferguson Case, an organised system and records existed – one factor that provided support that the cattle leasing arrangement in question was in fact a business.

Bowen CJ and Franki J in the Ferguson Case noted:

"…Organisation of activities in a businesslike manner, the keeping of books, records and the use of system may all serve to indicate that a business is being carried on."
