3. Hobby
Hobbies

Andre works full time as a chef. In his own time, he joins his friends in a jazz band as the singer. They play gigs at local bars; sometimes for a bar tab, a small amount of money, or just for fun.

When Andre contacted the ATO to ask about the income and expenses of his band work, he was told that based on the nature of his activities this was most likely a hobby rather than a business. The income was not assessable, nor would the expenses be allowed as a deduction.
What do you think?

If in future years, Andre’s jazz career takes off with more income, more gigs and less time as a chef. Will he have to declare any income from this activity?

    Yes
    No

Feedback

If you selected ’Yes‘, it is incorrect. The status of each activity needs to be determined for each income year.

If you selected ’No‘, you are correct. The status of each activity needs to be determined for each income year.
When a hobby becomes a business

Let us assume for a moment that Andre’s dream of jazz stardom came true.

At some point his hobby became a business for tax purposes - but at what point was that exactly?

While few businesses change into a hobby, many taxpayers invest in hobbies with the intention of building them into a business – with varying degrees of success.

It is, therefore, useful to build on your understanding of the indicators of business by taking time to define what makes a hobby and ensuring it is clear when a business commences and when it finishes.
Private Rulings

A taxpayer can now seek a private ruling on whether or not they are carrying on a business.

This applies to any provision as it relates to:

    liability
    administration
    procedure
    collection
    ultimate conclusion of fact.

The new provisions relating to private rulings are contained in Division 359 of Schedule 1 to the TAA1953 .

The provisions have applied from 1 January 2006.
Theory and Practice

Could you tell if a taxpayer has incorrectly classified a hobby as a business?

The line between entrepreneur and enthusiast can be slimmer than you may imagine.

There are times where the same type of activity may be a business to one taxpayer and a hobby to another.
Theory Track

Many of the cases you have previously reviewed illustrate broader principles about how taxpayer activities may be classified. Taxation rulings 97/11 and 2005/1 specifically address aspects of a hobby.
On-the-job resource

Taxation Ruling TR 97/11: Income tax: am I carrying on a business of primary production?  

Taxation Ruling TR 2005/1: Income tax: carrying on business as a professional artist  
Indicators of a hobby
Time

The more time spent on an activity, the more likely it is a business rather than a hobby.

In the Thomas Case   however, it was held that the taxpayer’s activity growing produce trees constituted the carrying on of a business, despite the fact that the time spent on the activity was minimal.

Other factors such as the commercial scale of the planting, expectation of a ready market and profitable return supported the conclusion that a business was carried on.
Concurrent activities

A taxpayer is more likely to be carrying on a business, if their activities are undertaken on a full time basis rather than a part time basis.

The Ferguson Case   however, established that the fact that a taxpayer is concurrently carrying on a profession or another business with an activity (such as cattle leasing) may not prevent the activity from amounting to the carrying on of a business.
Business name

If the taxpayer has registered a business name, the activity is more likely to be a business than a hobby.

By contrast, in the Crees Case  , the taxpayer registered a business name for his orchid growing activities but the activity was not found to be a business.

In this case, this factor was outweighed by other criteria relating to the commercial viability of the activity itself.
Comercial premises

An activity is more likely to be a business if it is run from a commercial premises rather than a home.

In the Evans Case  , one factor against the argument that the taxpayer’s horse and greyhound betting activities amounted to carrying on of a business was that that he had no office to work from.
Connection to other commercial activities

An activity is more likely to be a business if it is connected in some way to a taxpayer's other commercial activities.

For example, in considering if a gambling activity can be a business, one criterion is to determine whether the betting was related to or part of other activities of a businesslike character, such as horse breeding.
Market research

A taxpayer's activities are more likely to be a business, if research into the potential market for the taxpayer's product has occurred before the commencement of that activity.

In the Thomas Case  , after making some inquiries, the taxpayer believed there was a market for the macadamia and avocado pear trees he planted. He also believed that it would be profitable as demand would most likely exceed supply.
Possession of licence, qualification or special knowledge

If the taxpayer has the required licence, qualification or special knowledge, an activity is more likely to be a business than a hobby.

For example, a taxpayer's study in car design and building has been used as a factor to support his claim that he was in the motor racing and race car building business.

However, in the Shepherd Case , the taxpayer held a horse trainer's licence with the North & North Western District Racing Association, yet her horse training and betting activities were still considered to be a hobby.
Client base

If a taxpayer’s clients are the public at large, an activity is more likely to be a business. If clients are family and friends, this tends to suggest that the activity is merely a hobby.
Advertising

An activity that is advertised to entice potential customers, is more likely to be a business than a hobby.

The fact that the taxpayer advertised in the Hope Case , was one factor that suggested that his activities (agisting other people’s cattle and horses) amounted to a conducting a business.

In the Crees Case  however, the taxpayer's orchid activities did not constitute a business, although he had advertised in a magazine. This indicator alone was not enough to support the fact that a business was carried on, as the activity was not run in a businesslike manner and was incapable of producing a profit.
Employees

An activity is more likely to constitute the carrying on of a business, rather than a hobby, if staff members are employed for the activity.

For example, in the Thomas Case , where the taxpayer had planted commercial quantities of macadamia and avocado pear trees, paid labour was engaged from time to time.
Business plans

If a taxpayer has drawn up a business plan, it can be another indicator of carrying on a business.
