Case 1
The facts

A taxpayer, who is employed as an accountant, claims a deduction for depreciation and loss of disposal on a motor car. The car was used in connection with his motor racing activities. He purchased an old Holden for $50, then reconstructed it as a racing car at a cost of $1,500.

He then began taking part in competition race meetings for prize money. He attended most available meets over two income years and, as a result, won some prizes.

However, the total available prize money - had the taxpayer won every possible race outright - was $952 in the first year and $900 in the second.

Is this activity likely to be a hobby?
Weigh the indicators

Answer Yes or No to the following:

    Evident the taxpayer does not intend to make a profit?
    Motivated by pleasure not profit??
    Isolated transaction?
    Not carried on in the same manner as ordinary business?
    Small scale?
    Intention is to carry on a hobby, recreation or sport?
    Produce sold to family and friends rather than the public?
    Overall, is this a hobby?

The result
This is a hobby

These are the facts from Case C18 71 ATC 77.

In this case, the taxpayer was not carrying on a business of motor racing.

There were some factors present supporting that a business existed, but the activity was not able to produce a profit unless the taxpayer was successful, and even then the profit was only minimal.
Case 2
The facts

The taxpayer commenced motor racing as a hobby in the 1970s.

In the early 1980s, he built a racing car with the assistance of a large motor vehicle manufacturer.

He also received sponsorship from a printing company where he was employed. This included monthly payments, additional payments for special events and time away from normal duties for activities such as car preparation, testing and racing.

In 1989, the taxpayer ceased racing so that he could build a new racing car.

He moved away from work to another state to focus full-time on his motor racing. He purchased from the manufacturer a new standard road car in order to construct a new racing car.

Is this more likely to be a hobby?
Weigh the indicators

Answer Yes or No to the following:

    Evident the taxpayer does not intend to make a profit?
    Motivated by pleasure not profit??
    Isolated transaction?
    Not carried on in the same manner as ordinary business?
    Small scale?
    Intention is to carry on a hobby, recreation or sport?
    Produce sold to family and friends rather than the public?
    Overall, is this a hobby?

The result
This is NOT a hobby

These are the facts from AAT Case 9465 (1994) 28 ATR 1144 where the taxpayer’s motor racing activity was considered to be a business, rather than a sport or hobby.

There were a number of factors that supported that a business was being carried on, including that he:

    moved away from his work to another state to focus on full time motor racing
    was car designing and car building
    had experience
    had studied the topic
    was involved in the industry.

The taxpayer’s activities over the years prior to the years in question, and during the three years from 1989 to 1991, amounted to more than the pursuit of a hobby or an addiction to a sport.

The break from racing while constructing a new, more competitive racing car, was an integral and continuing part of the taxpayer’s business. The goal of establishing a reputation, sufficient to attract sponsorship and obtain contracts for car design and building, depended upon success in racing.

The Commissioner issued amended assessments for the 1989, 1990 and 1991 years, disallowing deductions.

Do you agree with the objection?

NOTE:a later objection was allowed in part Case 20/94 94 ATC 217.
Case 3
The facts

After his retirement from full time work, a taxpayer travelled overseas with his wife. The purpose of the trip was to interview a person and write his biography.

He also wanted to conduct research for a book on the costs likely to be encountered by Australian tourists visiting overseas countries.

The taxpayer’s motivation was his admiration for the subject of the biography, whom he had met during the war. While planning to travel to the United Kingdom to interview his subject, the taxpayer also decided to conduct research for his book on tourists’ costs.

In addition to going to the United Kingdom, the taxpayer and his wife travelled through North America, Europe and Hong Kong.

After completing a draft of the biography, the taxpayer forwarded it to a publishing company which made suggestions for changes. As he could not attract a publisher for the biography or for the travel book, the taxpayer finally arranged to publish both books himself.

He then claimed the whole of the costs of the United Kingdom visit, and half of the costs of the other travel as a deduction.

He claimed his wife’s expenses on the basis that her role was essential to the business operation because she transcribed notes and acted as navigator in the course of their travels.

Is this likely to be a hobby?
Weigh the indicators

Answer Yes or No to the following:

    Evident the taxpayer does not intend to make a profit?
    Motivated by pleasure not profit??
    Isolated transaction?
    Not carried on in the same manner as ordinary business?
    Small scale?
    Intention is to carry on a hobby, recreation or sport?
    Produce sold to family and friends rather than the public?
    Overall, is this a hobby?

The result
This is a hobby

These are the facts from Q112 83 ATC 576.

The taxpayer’s activities, as an author, were considered to be a hobby.

Before the taxpayer commenced his activities, he did not do any research to determine the demand for the books he wrote, nor did he approach a publisher.

The activity lacked commercial purpose and was not significantly carried out in order to make a profit. More than anything, the taxpayer’s admiration for the subject of his book was his drive. The taxpayer did the activities for pleasure and satisfaction.

Contrast this with Case N30 81 ATC 163 which is about a school teacher who published poetry books. The taxpayer’s claim was allowed in part.

It was noted in Case N30:

“;…The taxpayer spent long hours over many years in research and in the writing of his books as well as in the pursuit of activities concerned with their publication and sale. On the evidence those activities were considerable and systematic and organised to the extent that they should be regarded as amounting to the carrying on of a business and not merely ‘the vigorous pursuit of a pastime’…”
Case 4
The facts

A taxpayer is a keen gambler.

He spends his time punting; principally on horse racing, card games and two-up.

He also has a side interest in Australian Rules football.

For a number of years, the income from his gambling endeavours was his primary source of income.

The taxpayer seeks to claim losses, incurred by gambling over a three year period, as deductions against his assessable income.

He also seeks to carry forward the losses as deductions against assessable income for two forthcoming financial years.

Is this activity likely to be a hobby?
Weigh the indicators

Answer Yes or No to the following:

    Evident the taxpayer does not intend to make a profit?
    Motivated by pleasure not profit??
    Isolated transaction?
    Not carried on in the same manner as ordinary business?
    Small scale?
    Intention is to carry on a hobby, recreation or sport?
    Produce sold to family and friends rather than the public?
    Overall, is this a hobby?

The result
This is a hobby

These are the facts in Brajkovich v Federal Commissioner of Taxation (1989) 20 ATR 1570; 89 ATC 5227 (Brajkovich Case).

In this case, the judges found that a taxpayer who gambled heavily was not carrying on a business, instead he was indulging in his passion for large scale gambling.

In the Brajkovich Case, Pincus, French and Gummow JJ set out the following criteria to determine if a taxpayer is carrying on a business of gambling:

    whether the betting is conducted in a systematic, organised and businesslike way
    its scale, that is, the size of the wins and losses
    whether the betting is related to, or part of, other activities of a businesslike character, for example, breeding horses
    whether the bettor appears to engage in his activity principally for profit or principally for pleasure
    whether the form of betting chosen is likely to reward skill and judgment or depends purely on chance
    whether the gambling activity in question is of a kind which is ordinarily thought of as a hobby or pastime.

In the Brajkovich Case it is also noted:

    With the increase in spread betting and other sophisticated systems where people work in conjunction to manipulate odds the possibility of gambling being carried out in a more systematic, organised, businesslike manner and the likelihood of profit, may be increased. These forms of gambling may also rely more on skill and judgment, rather than pure chance.

Although these forms of gambling may not be specifically referred to in current ATO view documents, the same legal principles are equally applicable. The facts of each case need to be analysed to determine whether gambling constitutes a business or hobby.
Case 5
The facts

A taxpayer has grown fruit and vegetables on his seven acre property for the past 20 years.

Although he is employed full-time, he spends most of his spare hours working on his crops in partnership with his wife.

The crops are well-tended and the advice of State agricultural officers has been sought and applied.

The taxpayer has struck difficulties in marketing his crops as they were in too small a quantity to be graded and boxed for sale through normal commercial outlets.

Is this activity likely to be a hobby?
Weigh the indicators

Answer Yes or No to the following:

    Evident the taxpayer does not intend to make a profit?
    Motivated by pleasure not profit??
    Isolated transaction?
    Not carried on in the same manner as ordinary business?
    Small scale?
    Intention is to carry on a hobby, recreation or sport?
    Produce sold to family and friends rather than the public?
    Overall, is this a hobby?

The result
This is a hobby

These are some of the facts from Case K9 78 ATC 98.

In this case, the taxpayer's market gardening activities were not a business.

Despite over 20 years of operations, a profit was neither made nor expected.

There was no real belief that the orchard would become a paying proposition, therefore, the enterprise did not have a significant commercial purpose or character.

One factor considered was that much of his produce was sold to family and friends.
Case 6
The facts

The taxpayer was a clerk employed by the public service. He had always had an interest in playing and performing music.

He gained part-time employment playing in a band, but found the level of earnings from band work inadequate.

He thought greater rewards might be gained from creating and marketing his own compositions.

He pursued this for two tax years in the hope of being awarded a contract with a recording or publishing company. Losses of between $1,500 and $2,500 were incurred in each of these years.

Is this likely to be a hobby?
Weigh the indicators

Answer Yes or No to the following:

    Evident the taxpayer does not intend to make a profit?
    Motivated by pleasure not profit??
    Isolated transaction?
    Not carried on in the same manner as ordinary business?
    Small scale?
    Intention is to carry on a hobby, recreation or sport?
    Produce sold to family and friends rather than the public?
    Overall, is this a hobby?

The result
This is a NOT a hobby

These are some of the facts from Case R96 84 ATC 637.

Despite the fact that the absence of profits creates the impression that the taxpayer’s activities lacked a significant commercial purpose, he possessed an expectation that profit would eventually be earned. His methods were directed more to the making of profits than the deriving of pleasure.

The taxpayer was carrying on a business in each of the three years in question as the activities:

    were undertaken on a continuous and repetitive basis
    were carried on without interruption throughout the whole of the three year period
    were organised in a businesslike manner.

Taxation Ruling TR 2005/1: Income tax: carrying on business as a professional artist provides guidance on the principles to be applied in determining whether an artist is carrying on a business as a ‘professional artist’.
Share trading - Definitions
Share trader

Carries out business activities for the purpose of earning income from buying and selling shares.
Shareholder

The main factors indicating that a taxpayer is carrying on a share trading business are:

    the nature of activity and purpose of profit making
    repetition or frequency of transactions
    systematic, businesslike manner and maintenance of records
    volume of trading
    amount of capital invested.

It is not necessary to be carrying on a business of share trading to claim deductions and expenses relating to shares.

However, the way income and expenditure are dealt with in relation to shares varies depending on whether the taxpayer is a share trader (in business) or a share holder (non-business).
Case 7
The facts

The taxpayer was a medical practitioner in a small town who began engaging in fishing activities. He purchased a boat, trailer and winch. He previously accompanied other fishermen and was a skilled navigator.

The taxpayer fished with two other crew members. They were not given money but received some of the fish. The remaining fish were sold at a fish market, resulting in a small return.

The taxpayer began by spending around three weekends out of five away from his medical practice, fishing when the weather permitted him to. Work commitments, however, increased which led him to fish only on alternative weekends.

The taxpayer was also ill during the year in question. He took some time off to recover and during that time, had some holiday trips on the boat, sometimes with family and friends.

A loss was recorded from the fishing activities. The taxpayer claimed that this was due not only to the decreased time spent on the activity, but also due to particularly poor weather during that year.

In the following year, the boat and equipment were sold.

The taxpayer’s boat was smaller than an ordinary full time fisherman’s boat and the catch smaller than that of three men ordinarily fishing on weekends.

Is this likely to be a hobby?
Weigh the indicators

Answer Yes or No to the following:

    Evident the taxpayer does not intend to make a profit?
    Motivated by pleasure not profit??
    Isolated transaction?
    Not carried on in the same manner as ordinary business?
    Small scale?
    Intention is to carry on a hobby, recreation or sport?
    Produce sold to family and friends rather than the public?
    Overall, is this a hobby?

The result
This is a hobby

These are some of the facts from Case D54 72 ATC 333.

In this particular case, it was held that the taxpayer’s activities did not constitute a business.

Earning a profit in the particular year was not essential, when considering whether the activity constituted a business. Concurrent activities also did not prevent the activity amounting to the carrying on of a business.

Commercial considerations were important. The taxpayer's activities lacked continuity and a system. Furthermore, he was not able to carry out fishing operations in a similar fashion and style to other professional fishermen.

As a doctor, he was unable to avail himself of good weather at all times, to take the opportunity to catch fish, as other professional fishermen could.

