4. Commencement & Cessation
Commencement

Recent court decisions have tended to broaden the deductibility of expenses for establishing or winding up a business. In this section, we will briefly review relevant court decisions that demonstrate this trend.

Paragraph 69A of Taxation Ruling TR 2001/14: Income tax: Division 35 - non-commercial business losses states that a business has commenced if there is:

    purpose, intention and decision to commence a business
    acquisition of a business structure
    commencement of business operations.

Relevance

The time of commencement of a business is relevant as it may help to determine when assessable income is derived and when allowable deductions may be claimed.
Deductibility

Expenditure is now deductible if it is incurred:

    in relation to an existing business
    in relation to a business that used to be carried on (such as capital expenses incurred in order to cease a business, for example, legal costs to terminate the services of employees.)
    in relation to a business proposed to be carried on (such as costs of feasibility studies, market research or setting up a business entity.)
    as a shareholder, beneficiary or partner - to liquidate or deregister a company or to wind up a trust or partnership that had carried on a business.

From 25 September 2007, expenditure that is rendered not deductible under Division 250 of the ITAA 1997, cannot be deducted under section 40-880 of the ITAA 1997. 

Taxpayers can deduct 20% of allowable expenditure in the year they incur it and in each of the following four years.

Any recoupment of the expenditure is included in the taxpayer's assessable income.
Related information

For more information refer to: Income and deductions for business.  available from the ATO website.
Steele Case

The High Court in Steele v Deputy Commissioner of Taxation (1999) 197 CLR 459  (Steele Case), also broadened the scope of deductibility with the expenses, incurred prior to the commencement of business.

This case concerns the deductibility of interest on money borrowed to purchase land intended to be developed.

The claim for interest was incurred during a period in which no relevant assessable income was derived.

Based on the decisions in the Steele Case, the Commissioner identifies the circumstances where interest will be regarded as having a nexus with gaining or producing assessable income. This is in spite of the interest being incurred in a period prior to the derivation of the relevant assessable income.
Nexus

A nexus will exist prior to earning the relevant income where:

    the interest is not incurred too soon, is not preliminary or a prelude to the income earning activities
    it is not private and domestic
    the period of interest outgoings prior to the derivation of relevant assessable income is not so long, that the nexus between outgoing and assessable income is lost
    the interest is incurred for the purpose of gaining and producing assessable income
    continuing efforts are undertaken in pursuit of that end.

The Commissioner’s view is provided in Taxation Ruling TR 2004/4 : Income tax: deductions for interest incurred prior to the commencement of, or following the cessation of, relevant income earning activities.
Question

In this case, do you believe the taxpayer is entitled to claim a deduction on expenditure incurred while improving the land?

    Yes
    No

Feedback

If you chose Yes, that is incorrect - In fact, a deduction for the expenditure incurred by the taxpayer in improving the land was not allowable Southern Estates Pty Ltd v Federal Commissioner of Taxation (1967) 117 CLR 481 

If you chose No, that is correct - Southern Estates Pty Ltd v Southern Estates Pty Ltd v Federal Commissioner of Taxation (1967)  found the actual commencement of a business must be distinguished from activities which are merely preparatory to commencing a business
Ceasing to be in business

The time at which a business ends is a question of fact. A person does not necessarily cease to carry on a business merely because of an intention or decision.

The issue will be determined by examination of the taxpayer’s actions and the time trading actually ceases.

In general, expenses incurred after ceasing business operations are not deductible, as they are not incurred in the course of carrying on a business.

The issue of cessation of business was considered by the courts in the context of the continuing business test inAvondale Motors (Parts) Pty Ltd v Federal Commissioner of Taxation (1971) 2 ATR 312, 124 CLR 97  (Avondale Motors Case).
Avondale Motors Case

In this case, the taxpayer had carried on a business of dealing in motor vehicle spare parts and accessories. The assets and stock in trade were disposed of, the premises closed down, and the employees were paid out.

The remaining debts were assigned to the parent company, which in turn released the debt owed to it by the taxpayer. The unprofitable business was sold and a similar business was recommenced under new management. The Commissioner disallowed a claim for deductions for accumulated losses.

One of the questions on appeal was whether the taxpayer had continued to carry on a business. The court acknowledged that it is possible to continue to carry on a business where there are periods of inactivity due to the inherent nature of the activity or some temporary adversity.

Here the taxpayer’s activities had ceased completely and there was no intention of resuming the conduct of that business. The business, therefore, was found to have ceased.
Relevance

The time a business ceases is a relevant issue, as it may help determine whether a deduction may be claimed for certain expenditure.

From 1 July 2005, the changes to section 40-880 of the ITAA 1997  and decisions by the courts have broadened the scope of deductibility, with the cessation of business.

The addition of Division 250 of the ITAA 1997 , from 25 September 2007, also affects the cessation expenditure that is rendered not deductible under section 40-880 of the ITAA 1997http://atolaw/view.htm?docid=PAC/19970038/250-10 . The same conditions for claiming apply.

The question of the relevance of cessation of a business, particularly in relation to section 8-1 of the ITAA 1997, was reconsidered by the Full Federal Court in:

    Federal Commissioner of Taxation v Brown (1999) 43 ATR 1; 99 ATC 4600 (Brown Case) 
    Federal Commissioner of Taxation v Jones (2002) 49 ATR 188; 2002 ATC 4135 (Jones Case) 

Brown Case

In the Brown Case, the taxpayer and his wife purchased and ran a delicatessen in partnership for a number of years. To fund the purchase, the partnership took out a ten year loan in 1988. The delicatessen was sold in 1990 and the proceeds from the sale were not enough to finalise the loan.

The taxpayer and his wife continued to pay interest on the loan until the loan was paid off in 1995.

The issue was whether the taxpayer could claim a deduction for interest paid on this loan during the 1993 and 1994 income years. The Full Federal Court held that the interest payments were allowable as a deduction.

The cessation of business did not break the nexus between the carrying on of the business and incurring the interest liability. There was sufficient proximity between the interest payments and the business.
Jones Case

In the Jones Case, the taxpayer carried on a trucking and equipment hire business in partnership with her husband. In 1992, the taxpayer’s husband died and the taxpayer sold the partnership assets, but was unable to clear all the debts of the partnership. A loan with ANZ for $80,000 remained.

The taxpayer continued to make loan repayments and claimed deductions for the interest expenses incurred. The taxpayer also refinanced the loan to obtain a lower rate of interest and continued to claim deductions for the interest incurred.

The Full Federal Court allowed a deduction for the interest expense incurred by the taxpayer after the business had ceased. There must be sufficient proximity between the outgoing and the business operations (Brown Case). The nexus between the expense and the business was not broken by the refinancing of the loan.

