5. Course wrap-up

To determine whether an activity is a business, the definition of the term in section 995–1 of the ITAA 1997 is consulted. This definition alone cannot hope to encompass every activity a taxpayer may classify as a business.

This decision is important for tax purposes as business status determines whether income from the activity is assessable and allows certain deductions on a range of expenses.

Taxation Ruling TR 97/11 incorporates the ATO’s considerations for carrying on a business, but we also rely on case law to provide guidance.

Numerous criteria have evolved over time that can be used to determine whether an activity amounts to the carrying on of a business.

Each case differs, as each case is determined on its own facts. Weigh the facts of the case in combination to make a decision on whether or not a business, or alternatively a hobby, is carried on. This decision must be made for each income year of the life of the business.

The time that a business commences or ceases is also an important consideration. Recent court decisions have broadened the scope of deductibility when a business begins and ceases its operations.

