Course description
This course describes lodging GST returns, paying GST, getting refunds, correcting errors in GST returns and the time limit for claiming input tax credits.
Course details
•	Duration: 1 hour
•	Proficiency level: Foundation
•	Audience: All ATO staff
•	Prerequisites: None
Learning outcomes
At the end of this course, you will be able to:
•	Explain the rules for lodging GST returns.
•	Explain the rules for paying GST.
•	Explain the rules for getting a refund.
•	Explain the options for correcting GST errors. 
1. GST returns
Division 31 of the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999 (GST Act) contains the basic rules about lodging a GST return.
1.1 Lodgment requirements
If you are registered or required to be registered for GST, you must lodge a GST return for each tax period under subsection 31-5(1) of the GST Act. According to subsection 31-5(2), this requirement applies regardless of whether:
•	Your net amount for the tax period is zero.
•	You are liable for the GST on any taxable supplies that are attributable to the tax period.
Your net amount is calculated under section 17-5 of the GST Act as:
GST − input credits, plus or minus any adjustments
 More information
We don’t cover calculating your net amount any further in this course. For more information, refer to the L&D course GST net amounts and adjustments.
 Example 1: net amount is zero
An entity that is registered for GST has no sales or purchases in a particular tax period, resulting in a net amount of zero.
Despite having a net amount of zero, the business must lodge a GST return for that tax period.
 Example 2: GST payable
An entity that is registered for GST has sales that are subject to GST.
Since the business has made sales that are subject to GST, it must report the GST payable on a GST return for that tax period.
 
1.2 Additional GST returns
Subsection 31-20(1) of the GST Act provides that you must, if required by the Commissioner, lodge a further or fuller GST return for a tax period even if you have already lodged one.
For example, the Commissioner could direct you to lodge a GST return in your capacity as agent or trustee.
The information required in these returns may be modified to avoid unnecessary duplication of material that has already been provided.
1.3 Lodgment deadlines
The due date for lodging your GST return depends on whether you have:
•	quarterly tax periods
•	other tax periods.
Quarterly tax periods
Subsection 31-8(1) of the GST Act provides that the due date for your GST return is as follows.
Table 1: GST return due dates for quarterly tax periods
If this day falls within the quarterly tax period	Due date
1 September	28 October
1 December	28 February
1 March	28 April
1 June	28 July
The Commissioner may allow for an extension of time. Two-week extensions of 28 October, 28 April and 28 July deadlines may be allowed if you lodge and pay online. Extensions are also allowed where GST returns are lodged and prepared by tax agents.
 
Other tax periods
Subsection 31-10(1) of the GST Act provides that you must lodge your GST return for the tax period (other than a quarterly tax period):
•	on or before the 21st day of the month following the end of that tax period, or
•	within such further period as the Commissioner allows.
However, subsection 31-10(2) provides that if the tax period ends during the first 7 days of a month, you lodge your GST return:
•	on or before the 21st day of that month, or
•	within such further period as the Commissioner allows.
In both cases, you can request an extension if necessary.
1.4 GST return and content
The GST return is normally lodged using the business activity statement (BAS) or other approved forms. The BAS reports GST and other taxes, with the ATO providing a customised form based on your specific needs.
If you have nothing to report you can lodge your return by phone.
Business activity statement
According to subsection 31-15(1) of the GST Act, your GST return must be in the approved form.
In practice, the approved form for the GST return is the business activity statement (BAS).
You use the BAS to report your GST related transactions for the tax period and other transactions such as fringe benefits tax (FBT), pay as you go withholding (PAYGW), luxury car tax, fuel tax credits and wine equalisation tax.
The ATO has developed various business activity statements tailored for entities with different reporting obligations. The ATO sends you the relevant business activity statement, considering factors such as the frequency that you lodge your GST return and the types of taxes reported.
Other forms
The GST Act does not specify what the other forms are. However, the Explanatory Memorandum to the GST Act explains the Commissioner may allow an entity to lodge its return by telephone, which is an accepted practice.
You are only entitled to lodge a BAS by phone if you have a nil business activity statement. That is, you have nothing to report at any label in the BAS.
1.5 Electronic lodgment of GST returns
Section 31-25 states when and how businesses must lodge their GST returns electronically based on their turnover.
Mandatory electronic lodgment
Subsection 31-25(2) requires you to lodge your GST returns electronically if your GST turnover meets the electronic lodgment turnover threshold, unless the Commissioner approves otherwise.
The electronic lodgment turnover threshold is $20 million (subsection 31-25(4)). According to subsection 188-10(1), if your GST turnover is at least $20 million, you meet the threshold.
 Example 3: mandatory electronic lodgment
An entity has a GST turnover of $25 million. They must lodge their GST returns electronically. They cannot lodge paper returns unless they get special permission from the Commissioner.
Optional electronic lodgment
Subsection 31-25(1) of the GST Act allows you to lodge your GST returns electronically.
 Example 4: optional electronic lodgment
An entity has a GST turnover of less than $20 million. They can choose to lodge their GST returns electronically if they find it convenient.
Reporting on non-GST matters
Subsection 388-80 of Schedule 1 to the Taxation Administration Act 1953 (TAA), specifies that if you choose (or are required) to lodge your GST return electronically, you must also lodge all other amounts on your BAS electronically.
 
 Example 5: report on all amounts electronically
An entity lodges their GST return electronically.
They must also include all other amounts on their BAS electronically. This includes reporting on non-GST matters such as PAYGW, FBT, luxury car tax, fuel tax credits, and wine equalisation tax.
