2. GST payments
Under Division 33 of the GST Act you must pay GST if your net amount is more than zero.
Meaning of assessed net amount
Section 195-1 of the GST Act defines ‘assessed net amount’ as the net amount assessed for the tax period.
2.1 Payment deadlines
The due date for paying GST depends on whether you use:
•	quarterly tax periods
•	other tax periods.
Quarterly tax periods
Subsection 33-3(1) of the GST Act gives these due dates.
Table 2: GST payment due dates for quarterly tax periods
If this day falls within the quarterly tax period	Due date
1 September	28 October
1 December	28 February
1 March	28 April
1 June	28 July
For the quarterly tax period ending 30 September (which includes 1 September) the due date for payment is 28 October. You have nearly a month to do any paperwork, calculate your liability and pay the amount owed.
 
Other tax periods
Subsection 33-5(1) of the GST Act says the due date for other tax periods is on or before the 21st day of the month after the tax period ends. However, if the tax period ends in the first 7 days of a month, the due date is on or before the 21st day of that month.
 Example 6: payment due date
An entity with a GST turnover has monthly tax periods. They have until 21 November to pay their assessed net amount for the October monthly tax period.
 Reflection question 1: due dates
Look at the due dates for paying an assessed net amount and the due dates for lodging a GST return.
What do you notice and what is the significance?
Answer
The due dates are the same. This reflects that assessing your GST liability is done on a self-assessment basis.
2.2 Payment methods
Subsection 33-10(1) of the GST Act allows you to pay your assessed net amount electronically. Subsection 33-10(2) provides that if your GST turnover meets the electronic lodgment turnover threshold ($20 million), you must pay electronically. Subsection 188-10(1) provides that your GST turnover meets a threshold amount if it is at least equal to that threshold amount.
The ATO provides you with a range of payment methods.
The quickest and easiest ways to pay your BAS are:
•	BPAY
•	credit card through our online services.
 
Other payment options include:
•	electronic transfer
•	direct debit
•	Government EasyPay
•	in person at Australia Post
•	mail.
2.3 Payments of assessed GST on importations
Under paragraph 33-15(1)(a) of the GST Act, when you import goods, you pay the GST at the same time and in the same way as any customs duty.
However, there are specific rules outlined in the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Regulations 2019 (GST Regulations) that may allow you to defer your GST payment. The specific rules detail eligibility and payment schedules (paragraph 33-15(1)(b).
 More information
We don’t cover GST on importations any further in this course. For more information, refer to the L&D course GST and importations.
