4. GST refunds
When your assessed GST amount is negative, the Commissioner must pay you that amount as a refund. This is explained in section 35-1 of the GST Act.
However, the refund can be used to offset any tax debts you owe to the Commonwealth. This is according to subsection 35-5(1) of the GST Act.
The authority that allows the Commissioner to use any refund or credit against any tax debts owed comes from Division 3 of Part IIB of the TAA.
 Example 7: offsetting refund against other tax debts
A company has outstanding income tax liabilities of $22,000. For the latest tax period, its assessed net amount was a refund of $25,000.
The $25,000 refund was offset against the $22,000 outstanding debt, and the Commissioner paid the company a refund of $3,000.
Discretion not to offset refund
The Commissioner has the discretion not to offset a GST refund against another tax debt if:
•	the taxpayer is complying with an arrangement to pay that debt by instalments, or
•	the ATO has agreed to defer recovery of that debt.
Amending refund assessments
Subsection 35-5(2) of the GST Act describes the process where an entity receives a refund it was not entitled to and the assessment is amended.
If the amended assessed net amount exceeds the original amount, the difference is treated as GST payable.
This rule applies even if the amended assessed net amount is less than zero, which would normally mean a refund.
 
 Example 8: amended refund assessment
An entity lodges its GST return for the monthly tax period, showing a refund of $3,000. The Commissioner pays this amount to the entity.
Later, the Commissioner amends the assessment to a refund of $1,000, meaning the entity was overpaid by $2,000.
The $2,000 is treated as an assessed net amount payable.
4.1 When your entitlement to be paid a refund arises
Section 35-10 of the GST Act provides that your entitlement to be paid a refund arises when the Commissioner gives you notice of the assessment of your net amount for the tax period.
The TAA contains the rules about when the Commissioner gives a notice of assessment.
Under subsection 155-15(1) of Schedule 1 to the TAA, the Commissioner is treated as having made an assessment of your net amount when you lodge your GST return.
Under subsection 155-15(4) the Commissioner is treated as having given you a notice of assessment at the same time.
However, under the TAA, the Commissioner has the discretion to retain refunds for verification purposes.
