5. Refund not paid
Even if you are entitled to get a refund, there are times when:
•	the Commissioner will retain your refund
•	the GST Act prevents your refund.
5.1 Retention of refunds
Subsection 8AAZLGA(1) of the TAA says the Commissioner can retain a refund if:
•	You provided information that affects the refund amount, and it needs to be checked.
•	You asked the Commissioner to retain the refund until the information is checked, and you haven't withdrawn your request.
Subsection 8AAZLGA(2) says the Commissioner must consider the following things before retaining a refund:
•	the accuracy of the information
•	the possibility of fraud, intentional disregard of tax laws, or carelessness
•	the impact on your finances
•	protecting tax revenue and ensuring recoverability if information is wrong
•	the complexity of verifying the information
•	the duration the refund has been kept
•	actions already taken to verify the information
•	the sufficiency of information to make a decision
•	the consistency with prior information
•	any other relevant factors.
 Attention
These rules about retaining a refund apply to other taxes as well as GST.
5.2 Prevention of refunds
Section 142-1 of the GST Act says excess GST won’t be refunded if it would give you a windfall gain.
Section 142-10 states that any excess GST you passed onto someone else is considered payable and on a taxable supply, until you refund the passed-on GST to that person.
In other words, if you charged too much GST to a customer and sent that amount to the ATO, you can’t get a refund from the ATO until you refund your customer.
 More information
We don’t cover the rules about restricting refunds in detail in this course. For more information, refer to the L&D course Refunding excess GST.
