6. Correcting GST errors
You have 2 options for correcting an error you made in working out the net amount for an earlier period:
•	amend the assessment for the earlier tax period (there are time limits)
•	correct the error in a later GST return (provided you meet the eligibility requirements).
6.1 Amending an assessment
You can amend an earlier assessment if you do so within the period of review.
Your period of review:
•	starts on the day the Commissioner gives you a notice of assessment, and
•	ends 4 years from the day after the notice of assessment is given.
An assessment of your net amount is generally made on the day you lodge your GST return. The GST return is considered the notice of assessment given on the same day.
 More information
We don’t cover the rules about amending assessments in detail in this course. For more information, refer to the L&D course GST assessments and amendments.
6.2 Correcting errors in a later GST return
Under subsection 17-20(1) of the GST Act, the Commissioner may make a determination allowing you to work out your net amount in the:
•	circumstances specified in the determination
•	way specified in the determination.
In accordance with subsection 17-20(1), the Commissioner has issued the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) (Correcting GST Errors) Determination 2023 (LI 2023/32). This instrument allows an error, that has been made in working out a net amount for an earlier tax period, to be corrected by including the amount of the error in a GST return of a later tax period. 
Meaning of error
An error is a mistake you made in working out your net amount for a tax period that would (if it was the only mistake made in the tax period) have resulted in your assessed net amount for that tax period being:
•	overstated (a credit error), or
•	understated (a debit error).
Where subsection 29-10(4) or section 142-10 of the GST Act applies, there will be no error for the purposes of LI 2023/32 despite any mistake made. In such cases, the assessed net amount for the earlier tax period will not be overstated.
If your entitlement to an input tax credit has ceased under Division 93 there will be no error for the purposes of LI 2023/32.
 Example 9: error
On 15 April 2024, an entity found out it didn't include GST on some sales for the tax period ending 31 December 2023. They had already lodged their GST return on 21 January 2024.
There is an error for the purposes of LI 2023/32 because they have understated their GST payable (debit error).
Credit errors
LI 2023/32 defines a credit error as a mistake you made in working out your net amount for an earlier tax period that would, if it was the only mistake made in the tax period, have resulted in your assessed net amount for that earlier tax period being overstated.
Examples of credit errors are:
•	Reporting GST payable on taxable supplies twice.
•	Overstating the GST payable by reporting that GST on sales were $10,800 instead of the correct amount of $10,500.
•	Omitting or understating a decreasing adjustment or overstating an increasing adjustment.
 
Debit errors
LI 2023/32 defines a debit error as a mistake you made in working out your net amount for an earlier tax period that would, if it was the only mistake made in the tax period, have resulted in your assessed net amount for that earlier tax period being understated.
Examples of debit errors are:
•	Failing to report the GST payable on a taxable supply.
•	Understating GST payable by reporting GST on sales as $1,000 instead of the correct amount of $10,000.
•	Claiming input tax credits for an acquisition twice.
•	Omitting or understating an increasing adjustment or overstating a decreasing adjustment.
6.3 When you can correct a credit error
Section 6 of LI 2023/32 provides that you can correct a credit error in a later GST return if:
•	the error relates to an amount of GST, an input tax credit or any adjustments under the GST Act
•	the earlier tax period started on or after 1 July 2012
•	you lodge the GST return for the later tax period within the period of review for the assessment of the net amount of the earlier tax period
•	at the time of lodging your GST return for the tax period
-	the error does not relate to a matter that you have been notified is subject to a compliance activity, and was not made in working out your net amount for an earlier tax period that is subject to compliance activity, or
-	the error does relate to a matter that you have been notified is subject to a compliance activity but the Commissioner has notified you in writing that the error can be corrected under LI 2023/32, and
•	you have not corrected that error, to any extent, in working out your net amount for another tax period.
6.4 When you can correct a debit error
To correct a debit error:
•	the requirements of section 6 of LI 2023/32 apply, and
•	the requirements of section 7 apply.
 
The additional requirements of section 7 are:
•	the error was not a result of recklessness as to the operation of a GST law or intentional disregard of a GST law
•	the error is corrected in a GST return that is lodged within the debit error time limit that corresponds with your current GST turnover specified in Table 3, and
•	the net sum of the debit errors is less than the debit error value limit that corresponds with your current GST turnover specified in Table 3.
Table 3: Debit error limits
Current GST turnover	Debit error time limit	Debit error value limit
Less than $20 million	18 months after the due date of the GST return for the tax period in which the error was made.	$12,500
$20 million to less than $100 million	12 months after the due date of the GST return for the tax period in which the error was made.	$25,000
$100 million to less than $500 million	12 months after the due date of the GST return for the tax period in which the error was made.	$50,000
$500 million to less than $1 billion	12 months after the due date of the GST return for the tax period in which the error was made.	$100,000
$1 billion and over	12 months after the due date of the GST return for the tax period in which the error was made.	$560,000

 
 Example 10: debit error limits
Tim's Auto Pty Ltd has a current GST turnover of $5 million and lodges its GST returns quarterly. As part of its year-end review for the 2024 income year (conducted at the end of August 2024), it identified 2 debit errors made in the previous year:
•	an error of $7,000 that occurred in the September 2023 tax period, and
•	a second error of $8,000 that also occurred in the September 2023 tax period.
The errors were not as a result of recklessness or intentional disregard of the GST law and Tim's Auto Pty Ltd wanted to correct these errors in the September 2024 tax period. Therefore, the first 2 requirements of requirements of paragraph 7(a) and (b) of LI 2023/32 were satisfied.
However, as the net sum of the debit errors ($7,000 + $8,000 = $15,000) exceeded the relevant debit error value limit of $12,500, Tim's Auto Pty Ltd could only correct the debit errors up to $12,500 in the later GST return.
 Reflection question 2: debt error time limit start date
The start date for the debit error time limit is the date your GST return (that contained the error) was due rather than the date it was lodged.
Why do you think this is the case?
Answer
For fairness and consistency.
Requiring the debit error to be corrected in a GST return lodged within 12 or 18 months of the due date for lodging the GST return for the tax period in which the error was made ensures a consistent time limit applies regardless of whether the incorrect GST return was lodged on time or not.
By contrast, if the time limit was based on the lodgment date, taxpayers who lodged late would effectively extend the time to correct the error beyond 12 or 18 months from when the error was made.
 
