Course description
This course describes when an acquisition is creditable, and how you calculate your input tax credit.
Course details
•	Duration: 45 minutes
•	Proficiency level: Foundation
•	Audience: All ATO staff
•	Prerequisites: None
Learning outcomes
At the end of this course, you will be able to:
•	Determine if an acquisition is creditable.
•	Calculate the input tax credit on a creditable acquisition. 
1. Creditable acquisitions
Section 11-5 of the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999 (GST Act) provides that you make a creditable acquisition if:
•	you acquire anything solely or partly for a creditable purpose
•	the supply of the thing to you is a taxable supply
•	you provide, or are liable to provide consideration for the supply
•	you are registered or required to be registered.
 Attention
There are special rules that make an acquisition:
•	creditable even if all the requirements in section 11-5 are not met
•	non-creditable even if all the requirements in section 11-5 are met.
We outline these briefly in the ‘Special rules’ chapter in this product.
 Reflection question 1: creditable acquisition – important concepts
You have seen the definition of a creditable acquisition.
What would you say are the important concepts in the definition?
Answer
The important concepts are:
•	you acquire
•	solely or partly
•	creditable purpose
•	supply to you
•	taxable supply
•	you provide or are liable to provide
•	consideration
•	consideration for the supply
•	registered for GST
•	required to be registered for GST.
 
  Example 1: creditable and non-creditable acquisitions
Kim began a new business in Brisbane on 1 July last year. Kim’s business is selling travel and camping goods.
Kim was required to register for GST as of 1 January this year and did so.
Creditable acquisition
In January, Kim bought 10 sleeping bags on credit. The sale of the sleeping bags was a taxable supply.
Kim’s acquisition is a creditable acquisition as Kim:
•	bought the sleeping bags
•	bought the sleeping bags for their business which is for a creditable purpose
•	was liable to pay for the sleeping bags
•	was registered for GST at the time.
Not a creditable acquisition
In April, Kim bought a lightweight waterproof camping jacket for personal use from a regular supplier.
Kim’s acquisition is not a creditable acquisition as they bought the jacket for private purposes which is not a creditable purpose.
