2. Acquire
Paragraph 11-5(a) of the GST Act requires you to acquire something to make a creditable acquisition. Section 11-10 defines what is meant by an acquisition.
Subsection 11-10(1) provides that an acquisition is any form of acquisition whatsoever.
Subsection 11-10(2) provides examples of things that are acquisitions:
•	acquiring goods
•	acquiring services
•	receiving advice or information
•	accepting a grant, assignment or surrender of real property
•	accepting a grant, transfer, assignment or surrender of any right
•	acquiring a financial supply
•	acquiring the right to require another person
-	to do anything
-	to refrain from an act
-	to tolerate an act or situation.
An acquisition includes any combination of any 2 or more of those acquisitions.
Subsection 11-10(3) excludes 2 things from the meaning of acquisition:
•	An acquisition of money unless the money is provided as consideration for a supply that is a supply of money or digital currency
•	An acquisition of digital currency unless the digital currency is provided as consideration for a supply that is a supply of digital currency or money.
  Reflection question 2: meanings of acquisition and supply
You have seen the definition of an acquisition.
How does it compare with the definition of a supply?
Answer
Section 11-10 of the GST Act (meaning of acquisition) and section 9-10 (meaning of supply) are virtually identical. Though they are of course viewed from the other side of the transaction.
For every example of a supply, you have a similar example of an acquisition.
Like supply, the meaning of acquisition is very broad.
