3. Creditable purpose
  Reflection question 3: implication of the term ‘solely or partly’
An acquisition may be creditable if it is made solely or partly for a creditable purpose.
What do you think the implication of the term ‘solely or partly’ is?
Answer
It means an acquisition need not be fully for a creditable purpose for you to make a creditable acquisition and be entitled to an input tax credit.
It also means a single acquisition may have more than one purpose.
3.1 Meaning of ‘creditable purpose’
Section 11-15 of the GST Act defines the term ‘creditable purpose’.
Subsection 11-15(1) provides that you acquire something for a creditable purpose to the extent that you acquire it in carrying on your enterprise.
Enterprise (defined in section 9-20) includes more activities than a business, for example:
•	operating a business
•	leasing
•	the activities of a charity
•	the activities of a super fund.
One-off transactions can also be an enterprise. Certain activities are specifically excluded. These include the activities of an employee and a hobby.
 Attention
We will not be covering the meaning of enterprise in any more detail in this course.
 More information
For more information see the L&D course called GST enterprises.
3.2 Acquire in carrying on an enterprise
Paragraphs 54 to 100 of Goods and Services Tax Ruling GSTR 2008/1 Goods and services tax: when do you acquire anything or import goods solely or partly for a creditable purpose? explain our view on when you acquire something in carrying on an enterprise.
In many cases it is clear that there is a connection between the thing acquired and the enterprise being carried on. However, situations may arise where it is not clear. For example, it may not be clear whether a thing was acquired in carrying on the enterprise, or whether it is private or domestic in nature.
Whether something is acquired in carrying on an enterprise requires a connection or link between the thing acquired and the enterprise. It is a question of fact and degree. It is impractical to provide an exhaustive list of all the relevant factors.
However, some factors that would suggest an acquisition is made in carrying on an enterprise include:
•	The acquisition is incidental or relevant to the commencement, continuance or termination of the enterprise.
•	The thing acquired is used by the enterprise in making supplies.
•	The acquisition secures a real benefit or advantage for the commencement, continuance or termination of the enterprise.
•	The acquisition is one which an ordinary business person in the position of the recipient would be likely to make for the enterprise.
•	The acquisition does not meet the personal needs of individuals such as partners or directors.
•	The acquisition helps to protect or preserve the enterprise entity, structure or organisation.
•	the acquisition is made by the entity in accordance with, or to satisfy, a statutory requirement imposed on the enterprise.
There is also a connection between acquisitions of a capital nature and the enterprise being carried on. Although acquisitions of a capital nature are denied a deduction for income tax purposes, there is no similar denial of an input tax credit for GST purposes.
  Example 2: acquisitions made
A sole-trader operating a shoe shop acquires shoes to sell through their shoe shop. The acquisition is made in carrying on the enterprise.
The sole trader acquires the shoes to give as a gift to their child. The acquisition is not made in carrying on the enterprise.
  Example 3: acquisitions of a capital asset
A car manufacturing company purchases new spray-painting machinery for use on its production line.
The acquisition is made in carrying on its enterprise and is for a creditable purpose.
3.3 Excluded acquisitions
Subsection 11-15(2) of the GST Act provides that you do not acquire the thing for a creditable purpose to the extent that:
•	the acquisition is of a private or domestic nature, or
•	the acquisition relates to making supplies that are input taxed.
3.4 Private or domestic nature
Paragraphs 77 to 91 of GSTR 2008/1 explain our view on:
•	acquisitions that are private or domestic in nature
•	acquisitions that are commonly used for private or domestic purposes.
Paragraph 11-15(2)(b) of the GST Act does not operate independently from subsection 11-15(1). An acquisition that is of a private or domestic nature would rarely, if ever, be acquired in carrying on an enterprise.
Whether an acquisition is made in carrying on an enterprise or for some other purpose depends on all the facts and circumstances. It is not enough that an acquisition appears to be for the purposes of the enterprise or not.
  Example 4: acquisition not made in carrying on an enterprise
The owner of a book store buys a rare collector's book through the store's normal supply channels to give to their child for their birthday.
The acquisition is not made in carrying on the enterprise, although it may appear to be so from a superficial examination of the circumstances of the purchase.
 
3.5 Making input taxed supplies
Under paragraph 11-15(2)(a) of the GST Act, an acquisition is not acquired for a creditable purpose to the extent that the acquisition relates to making supplies that would be input taxed.
Division 40 covers input taxed supplies and describes when a supply is input taxed. Input taxed supplies include:
•	financial supplies
•	sales of residential premises
•	renting residential premises
•	supplies by school tuckshops.
Paragraphs 101 to 196 of GSTR 2008/1 explain our view on determining a connection between an acquisition and making supplies that would be input taxed.
Paragraph 11-15(2)(a) of the GST Act does not require tracing to a specific supply. However, it does require some form of connection to the supplies the entity makes, made or intends to make.
Paragraph 120 of GSTR 2008/1 lists some situations that raise particular considerations in establishing a connection between the acquisition and the making of supplies that would be input taxed.
The situations include:
•	An acquisition is used or consumed in making input taxed supplies. For example, materials used in repairing residential premises that are leased.
•	An acquisition is used to promote, market or advertise the making of input taxed supplies (for example, a bank promoting its loan products).
•	An acquisition is made in the course of *making an acquisition-supply.
•	An acquisition is an overhead of the enterprise, or segment of the enterprise, that is making supplies that would be input taxed (for example, rental of premises by a bank that provides both taxable and input taxed supplies).
•	An acquisition is preparatory to the making of an input taxed supply
•	An acquisition is made as a consequence of an input taxed supply that has already been made (past supplies).
•	Acquisitions made in the course of raising capital (other than by borrowing).
 
  Example 5: acquisition used to make input taxed supplies
Stack Trucks sells trucks to customers and also offers them finance by way of loans.
Stack Trucks acquires a specialist loan management software which is used exclusively by loans staff. The acquisition is made in carrying on Stack Trucks' enterprise. The software is used wholly in making input taxed supplies.
The acquisition of the software is therefore not for a creditable purpose.
3.6 Exceptions to the ‘input taxed supplies’ rule
Section 11-15 of the GST Act provides for 3 such exemptions. That is, circumstances in which an acquisition is not treated as relating to making an input taxed supply.
These are for:
•	enterprises carried on outside the indirect tax zone
•	entities that do not exceed the financial acquisitions threshold
•	borrowings to make supplies that are not input taxed.
Enterprises carried on outside the indirect tax zone
Subsection 11-15(3) of the GST Act provides that an acquisition is not treated as relating to an input taxed supply to the extent that the supply is made through an enterprise that you carry on outside the indirect tax zone.
Paragraphs 198 and 199 of GSTR 2008/1 explain our view on this exception.
 Reflection question 4: enterprises carried on outside the indirect tax zone
An acquisition is not treated as relating to making an input taxed supply if the supply is made through an enterprise that the entity carries on outside the indirect tax zone.
Why do you think this rule exists?
Answer
The policy principle of the provision is to ensure that GST is not borne on exports or other supplies that are made outside the indirect tax zone.
The GST is intended to apply to supplies ‘consumed’ in the indirect tax zone.
Not exceeding the financial acquisitions threshold
Subsection 11-15(4) of the GST Act provides that an acquisition is not treated as relating to an input taxed supply if:
•	it relates to making financial supplies, and
•	you do not exceed the financial acquisitions threshold.
Paragraphs 200 to 202 of GSTR 2008/1 explain our view on this exception.
Paragraph 202 of GSTR 2008/1 refers you to Goods and Services Tax Ruling GSTR 2003/9 Goods and Services Tax: financial acquisitions threshold which explains our view on when an entity exceeds the financial acquisitions threshold.
Division 189 of the GST Act contains the rules about determining if you exceed the financial acquisitions threshold.
 Attention
We will not be covering the rules in any further details in this product.
 More information
For more information, refer to
•	the L&D course called GST and financial supplies
•	Division 189 of the GST Act
•	GSTR 2003/9.
Borrowings to make supplies that are not input taxed
Subsection 11-15(5) of the GST Act provides that an acquisition is not treated as relating to an input taxed supply if:
•	it relates to making a financial supply that is a borrowing (other than through a deposit account), and
•	the borrowing relates to you making supplies that are not input taxed.
Paragraphs 203 to 214 of GSTR 2008/1 explain our view on this exception.
The purpose of subsection 11-15(5) is to ensure an acquisition that relates to a financial supply consisting of a borrowing is made for a creditable purpose if the borrowing relates to supplies that are not input taxed.
  Example 6: borrowing to make supplies that are not input taxed
Juggernaut Pty Ltd is a truck manufacturer. It acquires legal and accounting services as part of entering into a loan agreement with Big Bank. The funds borrowed by Juggernaut Pty Ltd are used to upgrade manufacturing equipment.
Juggernaut acquires the legal and accounting services in carrying on Juggernaut's enterprise that relate to making a financial supply consisting of a borrowing (taking out the loan).
As the borrowing relates to the making of taxable supplies of the trucks, the acquisition of the legal and accounting services is for a creditable purpose.
