5. Consideration
Paragraph 11-5(c) of the GST Act requires that you provide or are liable to provide consideration for the supply.
Paragraph 181 of Goods and Services Tax Ruling GSTR 2006/9 Goods and services tax: supplies explains that 2 of the requirements for making a creditable acquisition are that you:
•	are the recipient of the supply, and
•	provide or are liable to provide consideration for the supply.
The term ‘liable to provide’ means you don’t always have to actually provide the consideration. For example, you may make a creditable acquisition even if a third party (such as your agent) provides the consideration.
5.1 Meaning of consideration
The definition of consideration is intended to be very broad. It extends beyond payments.
Subsection 9-15(1) provides that consideration includes:
•	any payment, act or forbearance in connection with a supply of anything
•	any payment, act or forbearance in response to, or for, inducing a supply of anything.
Subsections 9-15(2) and (2A) provide that it does not matter if the payment, act or forbearance was:
•	voluntary or not
•	by the recipient or not
•	in compliance with an order of a court or similar body
•	in compliance with a settlement relating to proceedings before a court or similar body.
Paragraph 12 of Goods and Services Tax Ruling GSTR 2001/6 Goods and services tax: non-monetary consideration explains our view that payment is not limited to a payment of money. It includes a payment in a non-monetary form such as providing goods.
 
 Attention
We will not be covering the meaning of consideration in any more detail in this course.
 More information
For more information, refer to the L&D course called GST and taxable supplies.
5.2 Consideration for the supply
Paragraph 11-5(a) of the GST Act provides that the consideration you provide must be for the supply made to you. In other words, there must be connection or nexus between the consideration and the supply.
Paragraph 180 of GSTR 2006/9 explains that in determining whether a payment is consideration under section 9-15 and whether there is a 'supply for consideration', our view is:
•	the test is whether there is a sufficient nexus between the supply and the payment made (this test is objective)
•	regard needs to be had to the true character of the transaction
•	an arrangement between parties will be characterised not merely by the description that the parties give to the arrangement, but by looking at all of the transactions entered into and the circumstances in which the transactions are made.
 Attention
We will not be covering the nexus between the consideration and the supply in any more detail in this course.
