7. Input tax credits
Section 11-20 of the GST Act provides that you are entitled to the input tax credit for any creditable acquisition that you make.
The method you use to calculate your input tax credit depends on whether your acquisition is fully creditable or not.
7.1 Fully creditable acquisitions
Section 11-25 of the GST Act provides that the amount of your input tax credit for a fully creditable acquisition is equal to the GST payable on the supply of the thing you acquire.
  Example 7: calculating the input tax credit
Bounce Pty Ltd (Bounce) runs a fun park. They bought 10 trampolines to use in their business for $6,600 (including $600 GST). Their acquisition was fully creditable.
Bounce is entitled to an input tax credit of $600.
 Attention
We will not be covering how to calculate the GST payable in this course.
 More information
For more information, refer to the L&D course called GST and taxable supplies.
7.2 Partly creditable acquisitions
Section 11-25 of the GST Act provides that your input tax credit is reduced if your acquisition is only partly creditable.
Subsection 11-30(1) provides that your input tax credit is reduced if either or both of the following apply:
•	You make the acquisition only partly for a creditable purpose.
•	You provide, or are liable to provide, only part of the consideration.
Subsection 11-30(3) contains the formula for calculating your input tax credit for partly creditable acquisitions.
Full input tax credit × Extent of creditable purpose × Extent of consideration
Where:
•	Extent of consideration is the extent to which you provide, or are liable to provide, the consideration for the acquisition, expressed as a percentage of the total consideration for the acquisition.
•	Extent of creditable purpose is the extent to which the creditable acquisition is for a creditable purpose, expressed as a percentage of the total purpose of the acquisition.
•	Full input tax credit is what would have been the amount of the input tax credit for the acquisition if it had been made solely for a creditable purpose and you had provided, or had been liable to provide, all of the consideration for the acquisition.
Paragraph 45 of GSTR 2006/4 explains it is an entity’s planned usage that is used to determine the extent of its creditable purpose.
  Example 8: calculating the input tax credit
Jamila runs a business making and selling frames for pictures, mirrors and so on. Jamila is a sole trader and is registered for GST.
Jamila acquires 50 metres of timber for $110 (including $10 GST).
Jamila intends to use most of the acquisition for making frames for customers. However, they also intend to use 5 metres of the timber for making a mirror frame as a birthday present for their mother.
Jamila therefore acquired 90% of the timber for a creditable purpose and 10% for a private purpose.
Jamila's input tax credit is $10 × 90% = $9
