Course description
This course describes how goods and services tax (GST) assessments and GST amendments are made.
•	Duration: One hour
•	Proficiency level: Foundation
•	Audience: All ATO staff
•	Prerequisites: None
Learning outcomes
At the end of this course, you will be able to:
•	Explain how we make GST assessments.
•	Explain how we make GST amendments.
Related courses
This course is one of a set of courses aligned to the GST general knowledge requirements in the Capability Framework.
Within this set of general knowledge requirements, there are 5 core requirements. We recommend that you complete these related L&D courses (in the listed order) to give you a basic understanding of GST fundamentals:
1.	GST – an overview
2.	GST enterprises
3.	Registering for GST
4.	GST and taxable supplies
5.	GST and creditable acquisitions.
1. Assessed net amounts
1.1 Payments and refunds
Divisions 33 (payments of GST) and 35 (refunds) of the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999 (GST Act) both refer to assessed net amounts.
These are used to determine if you either:
•	must pay an amount to the ATO (where your assessed net amount is greater than zero), or
•	get a refund (if your assessed net amount is less than zero).
 Attention
We will not cover payments of GST and GST refunds in any more detail in this course.
Section 195-1 of the GST Act defines assessed net amount as the net amount assessed for a tax period.
Section 195-1 defines assessment by referring to the Income Tax Assessment Act 1997 (ITAA 1997). Section 995-1 of the ITAA 1997 defines an assessment of an assessable amount as an ascertainment of the assessable amount.
Subdivision 155-A of Schedule 1 to the Taxation Administration Act 1953 (TAA) contains the rules for making assessments for tax periods.
2. Making assessments
2.1 Commissioner may make an assessment
Subsection 155-5(1) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the Commissioner may make an assessment of an assessable amount (including an assessable amount of zero).
An assessment is the determination of the liability of a taxpayer under a taxation law including GST.
An assessment crystallises the amount the taxpayer is required to pay or get as a refund.
 Attention
Amounts not included in an assessment are not required to be paid but remain payable.
Subsection 155-5(2) lists the assessable amounts, which include:
•	a net amount
•	an amount of indirect tax not included in an amount covered by another paragraph of the subsection
•	a credit under an indirect tax law not included in an amount covered by another paragraph of the subsection.
Under section 155-5, the Commissioner can make an assessment at any time, however:
•	the Commissioner may be required to make an assessment if they have not issued a notice of assessment within 6 months of a taxpayer lodging their GST return
•	there are restrictions about the time allowed to amend an assessment.
 More information
For more information see ‘Delays in making amendments’ and ‘Making amendments’ in this product.
The Commissioner is deemed to make an assessment (under section 155-5) where taxpayers self-assess the assessable amount under sections 155-15 or 155-20.
 
If a taxpayer does not self-assess under sections 155-15 or 155-20, the Commissioner can make a default assessment (under section 155-5) based on the information that they have available. The Commissioner must have reasonable grounds for making the assessment.
 Reflection question 1: Assessments of GST payable on a taxable importation
Look at the assessable amounts the Commissioner can make an assessment on.
Do you think the Commissioner can make an assessment of the GST payable on a taxable importation?
Answer
Yes.
The GST payable on a taxable importation is ‘an amount of indirect tax that is not included in a net amount or net fuel amount’.
2.2 Making an assessment under the self-assessment rules
Assessment of net amounts
Most entities will be able to self-assess under section 155-15 of Schedule 1 to the TAA.
Subsection 155-15(1) provides that the Commissioner is treated as having made an assessment (under section 155-5) of your net amount for the tax period if you send your GST return to the Commissioner (that is, you lodge your GST return).
Subsection 155-15(2) provides that the Commissioner is treated as having made the assessment on the day that you do so.
 Example 1: Deemed assessment
Alex runs a furniture shop and is registered for GST.
On 28 October 2023, Alex lodges their GST return for the tax period ending 30 September 2023.
The Commissioner is taken to have made an assessment of Alex's net amount for the tax period ending 30 September 2023, on 28 October 2023.
Assessment of taxable importations
Subsection 155-15(1) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the Commissioner is treated as having made an assessment (under section 155-5) of your GST payable on a taxable importation if your return, is given as described in one of the following provisions, in relation to the importation:
•	paragraph 69(8)(a), (b) or (c) or (da), or 70(7)(a) of the Customs Act 1901 (Customs Act)
•	regulations prescribed for the purposes of paragraph 69(8)(d) of that Act.
The return must be given to:
•	the Collector (within the meaning of the Customs Act), or
•	the Department administered by the Minister administering Part XII of that Act.
Subsection 155-15(2) provides that the Commissioner is treated as having made the assessment on the day that you do so.
2.3 Making an assessment for a taxable importation
 Attention
For the purposes of this course, we cover how section 155-20 of Schedule 1 to the TAA applies to the GST payable on a taxable importation. However, section 155-20 also applies to luxury car tax, wine tax, taxable importations of luxury cars and customs dealings.
Subsection 155-20(1) provides that the Commissioner is treated as having made an assessment (under section 155-5) of GST payable on your taxable importation if:
•	the document mentioned in the first column of the following table is communicated to the Department administered by the Minister administering Part XII of the Customs Act, in respect of the importation, and
•	a Collector (within the meaning of the Customs Act) gives the related document mentioned in the second column to an entity in respect of the importation.
Document communicated	Document given to an entity
An import declaration	An import declaration advice
A self-assessed clearance declaration (within the meaning of the Customs Act)	A self-assessed clearance declaration advice
Section 155-20(2) provides that the Commissioner is treated as having made the assessment on the day a Collector gives the document mentioned to the entity.
 Example 2: Deemed assessment
Bayan imports a bicycle and lodges an import declaration with the Department on 1 September 2023.
The collector issues Bayan an import declaration advice on 2 September 2023.
The GST payable is deemed to be assessed on that date.
2.4 One assessment per tax period
Subsection 155-15(5) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that section 155-15 (making assessments and self-assessment) does not apply to an assessable amount if the Commissioner has already assessed that amount on or before the day mentioned in paragraph 155-15(4)(b).
Subsection 155-20(5) provides that section 155-20 (making assessments and assessments of indirect tax on importations and customs dealings) does not apply if the Commissioner has already assessed the GST on or before the day mentioned in paragraph 155-20(4)(b).
In other words, an entity cannot self-assess if an assessment has already been made, whether it was deemed or initiated by the Commissioner.
A declaration lodged in accordance with section 155-20 cannot result in a deemed assessment if the Commissioner has already assessed the GST on or before the day a collector gives an import declaration advice. This may occur where a taxpayer has provided insufficient documents to the Department for a liability arising on a taxable importation so the Commissioner issues a notice under section 155-10.
Any subsequent amendments made by the Commissioner (including those instigated by the entity) are amendments of the assessment. Therefore, the rules for amending assessments (in Subdivision 155-B) apply.
 Example 3: Only one assessment
Cassidy operates a bookstore and is registered for GST.
On 28 October 2023, Cassidy lodges their GST return for the tax period ending 30 September 2023.
On 23 March 2024, Cassidy lodges an amended GST return for the tax period ending 30 September 2023. The amended return lodged by Cassidy will be treated as an application for an amendment by the Commissioner.
Cassidy fails to lodge their GST return for the tax period ending 31 March 2024 and the Commissioner makes an assessment for that tax period on 23 June 2024.
On 25 June 2024, Cassidy lodges their GST return for the same tax period.
That GST return will be treated by the Commissioner as an application by Cassidy for an amendment of the assessment made on 23 June 2024.
2.5 Returns that are not deemed assessments
Some returns lodged by entities do not contain enough information to allow a deemed assessment to occur.
For example, GST returns lodged by certain entities with liabilities under:
•	Division 78 of the GST Act (Insurance)
•	Division 105 of the GST Act (Supplies in satisfaction of debts).
Also, some documents given by entities to Customs for liabilities arising on taxable importations.
For these entities, the Commissioner must make an assessment (under the general power in section 155-5 of Schedule 1 to the TAA) before the entity's liabilities or entitlements are crystallised.
2.6 Special assessments
Section 155-25 of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the Commissioner may treat part of a tax period as the whole tax period for the purposes of making an assessment (under section 155-5) of an assessable amount that relates to a tax period.
Usually, all transactions undertaken during a tax period (for example, a month) are taken into consideration when determining an assessable amount.
However, in some circumstances the Commissioner may be prevented from including all transactions. In such circumstances section 155-25 allows the Commissioner to calculate the assessable amount for only part of the period.
This allows assessments to be made in relation to non-standard tax periods, like a GST-registered entity's first or final tax period.
 Example 4: Backdating GST registration
Bo starts a sole trader business on 1 July 2017 and decides not to register for GST.
Bo genuinely believes they do not make any taxable supplies and therefore does not lodge any GST returns.
On 15 June 2023 the Commissioner determines that Bo should have been registered from 1 July 2017.
However, as there has been no fraud or evasion, Bo's registration cannot be backdated more than 4 years. That is, Bo's registration can only be backdated to 16 June 2019.
The Commissioner will make a special assessment for the part period between 16 June 2019 and 30 June 2019.
2.7 Delays in making assessments
Subsection 155-30(1) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that you may give the Commissioner a written notice requiring the Commissioner to make an assessment if:
•	you have given the Commissioner the relevant return (if any) for an assessable amount
•	6 months has passed since you did so, and
•	the Commissioner has not given you a notice of assessment.
Under section 155-10, the Commissioner must give a notice of assessment as soon as possible. However, delays in making assessments could occur in circumstances where there is no deemed assessment under section 155-15 or 155-20.
Subsection 155-30(2) provides that you may object (in the manner set out in Part IVC of the TAA) if the Commissioner does not make the assessment within 30 days after the day you give the notice under subsection 155-30(1).
 
 Example 5: Written notice and objection
Cal is not registered for GST.
Cal lodges a return claiming fuel tax credits on 14 October 2022.
On 15 April 2023 Cal gives the Commissioner a written notice requesting an assessment of the fuel tax return lodged s6 months earlier.
If the Commissioner has not issued Cal with a notice of assessment by 15 May 2023, Cal has 60 days to lodge an objection under Part IVC.
