3. Giving a notice of assessment
3.1 Commissioner must give a notice of assessment
Subsection 155-10(1) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the Commissioner must give an entity a notice of assessment of the entity’s assessable amount as soon as practicable after the assessment is made.
 Attention
The Commissioner’s obligation to give a notice of assessment is limited to assessments of assessable amounts listed in subsection 155-5(2).
The Commissioner must give a notice of assessment to:
•	complete the assessment process
•	allow taxpayers to seek an amendment of the assessable amount within the period of review.
Taxpayers that self-assess are deemed to have been issued with a notice of assessment when they lodge certain documents with the Commissioner or Customs. When this happens, the requirement that the Commissioner give a notice of assessment is met.
Generally, non-business taxpayers and persons not registered for GST will be given a notice of assessment by the Commissioner.
Under section 28A of the Acts Interpretation Act 1901, the notice may be given by:
•	delivering it personally to the taxpayer’s last known address
•	leaving it at the taxpayer’s last known address
•	sending it by pre-paid post, to the taxpayer’s last known address.
Under section 29, where it is sent by pre-paid post it is deemed to be given at the time the letter would be delivered in the ordinary course of post.

There is no time limit for issuing a notice of assessment to a taxpayer other than it must be done as soon as practical. ‘As soon as practical’ is not defined and therefore takes its ordinary meaning. However, if a notice of assessment has not been received within 6 months, the taxpayer may require the Commissioner to make an assessment.
 Example 6: Notice of assessment given to a business taxpayer
Ehsan is registered for GST and lodges an activity statement.
The activity statement (GST return) is a deemed notice of assessment.
 Example 7: Notice of assessment given to a non-business taxpayer
Alexi is not registered for GST but is eligible to claim fuel tax credits. Alexi lodges a claim form with the ATO.
The Commissioner will issue a notice of assessment.
3.2 Giving a notice of assessment under the self-assessment rules
Notice of assessment for net amounts
Subsection 155-15(4) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that your GST return is treated as a notice of assessment:
•	signed by the Commissioner, and
•	given to you (under section 155-10) on the day you lodge your GST return.
 Example 8: Notice of assessment under self-assessment rules
Aarya runs a furniture shop and is registered for GST.
On 28 October 2023, Aarya lodges their GST return for the tax period ending 30 September 2023.
The GST return is a notice of assessment issued by the Commissioner on 28 October 2023.
 
Notice of assessment for taxable importations
Subsection 155-15(4) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that your return is treated as a notice of assessment:
•	signed by the Commissioner, and
•	given to you (under section 155-10) on the day you give your return to the recipient.
The recipient is either
•	the Collector, or
•	the Department administered by the Minister administering Part XII of the Customs Act.
 Example 9: Deemed assessment
Angel imports a bicycle and lodges an import declaration with the Department on 1 September 2023.
The collector issues Angel an import declaration advice in respect of the importation on 2 September 2023.
The import declaration and the import declaration advice are collectively taken to be a notice of assessment given to Angel on 2 September 2023 by the Commissioner.
Giving a notice of assessment for a taxable importation
Subsection 155-20(4) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the communicated and given documents (listed in table in subsection 155-20(1)) are together treated as being a notice of the assessment:
•	signed by the Commissioner, and
•	given to you (under section 155-10) on the day a Collector gives the relevant document to the entity.
