5. Making amendments
5.1 Initiating an amendment
An assessment can be amended either:
•	on the Commissioner's own initiative, or
•	at the request of the taxpayer entity.
5.2 An amendment is an assessment
Section 155-80 of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that an amended assessment of an assessable amount is an assessment for all purposes of any taxation law.
In other words, an amendment is an assessment.
 Reflection question 2: Assessments are amendments
Section 155-80 provides that an amendment of an assessment is an assessment.
What do you think the implications of this are?
Answer
It means that any legislation which applies to an assessment of an assessable amount also applies to the amendment of an assessable amount.
Note: Although an amendment is an assessment, there are additional requirements for amendments under Subdivision 155-B.
5.3 Notices of amended assessments
The note to subsection 155-10(1) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the section applies to amendments. This is in line with section 155-80 providing that amendments are assessments.
In other words, the Commissioner must give a notice for an amended assessment.
 
Certain amendment applications taken to be notices
Subsection 155-40(1) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that if you apply for an amendment of an assessment, the application is treated as being a notice of the amended assessment (given to you by the Commissioner under section 155-10) if:
•	your application is in the approved form
•	the Commissioner makes the amendment
-	to give effect to the decision on your application
-	during the period of review for the assessment, and
•	the amendment the Commissioner makes is the entire amendment for which you applied, and nothing else.
An approved form is defined to mean a return, notice, statement, application or other document that satisfies the requirements set out in section 388-50. For example, a business activity statement (BAS) is the approved form for lodging a GST return.
 Example 11: Deemed notice of assessment
Bhavya is registered for GST and lodges their 30 September 2023 activity statement on 28 October 2023.
Bhavya lodges a revised activity statement on 1 April 2024 decreasing the amount of input tax credits.
The ATO accepts the revised activity statement which is a deemed notice of amended assessment.
The amended approved form is not a deemed assessment if the Commissioner:
•	does not accept the amendment of the assessable amount in full, or
•	makes further amendments.
Instead, the Commissioner may be required to issue a notice of amended assessment as soon as practical.
 Example 12: No deemed notice of amended assessment
Alyn is registered for GST and lodges their September quarterly activity statement on 28 October 2018.
On 27 October 2023, Alyn lodges a revised GST return increasing the amount of input tax credits by $2,000.
The Commissioner does not make the amendment shown on the amended GST return.
The revised GST return is not a deemed notice of amended assessment.
Instead, the Commissioner may issue a notice of amended assessment restating the assessable amount shown in the original activity statement.
The time the notice is given
Subsection 155-40(2) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the notice is treated as having been given to you on whichever of the following is applicable:
•	the first day the Commissioner adjusts the balance of a running balance account (RBA) of yours as a result of the amendment, or
•	the day a Collector (within the meaning of the Customs Act) gives an import declaration advice, or a self-assessed clearance declaration advice, to an entity in respect of the relevant taxable importation as a result of the amendment.
Deeming the notice of amended assessment to be made from the day the RBA is adjusted allows the Commissioner to make a different amendment if they disagree with the amendment applied for.
 Example 13: Date of deemed notice of amended assessment
Atawhai is registered for GST and lodges their September quarterly activity statement on 28 October 2023.
On 14 May 2024, Atawhai lodges a revised GST return which increases their entitlement to input tax credits by $100.
The Commissioner makes the amendment for the exact amount shown on the revised GST return.
The credit of $100 is recorded on Atawhai’s RBA on 22 May 2024 which is during the period of review.
The revised GST return lodged by Atawhai is deemed to be a notice of amended assessment dated 22 May 2023.
Note: If only $50 had been credited to Atawhai’s RBA, the Commissioner would have been required to issue a notice of amended assessment when the amendment was made.
5.4 Period for making an amendment
The Commissioner may make an amendment:
•	within the period of review
•	at any time, if one of the exceptions (to the period of review time limit) apply.
The date of the original assessment is central to the time limits on the Commissioner's power to amend an assessment.
 More information
For more information, see ‘Amendments during the period of review’ and ‘Amendments after the period of review’ in this product.
Although there is a time limit for making amendments (the period of review), the Commissioner can make multiple amendments within the period of review.
5.5 Overpaid amounts
Section 155-75 of Schedule 1 to the TAA is about amendments that reduce a tax liability.
Subsection 155-75(1) provides that section 155-75 applies if:
•	an assessment of yours is amended, and
•	as a result of the amendment, a tax-related liability (the earlier liability) of yours is reduced.
Subsection 155-75(2) provides that for the purposes of any taxation law that applies the general interest charge, the amount by which the tax-related liability is reduced is taken never to have been payable.
Subsection 155-75(3) provides that the Commissioner must apply the amount of any overpaid tax-related liability in accordance with Divisions 3 and 3A of Part IIB of the TAA (about running balance accounts and the application of payments and credits).
The overall effect of subsections 155-75(1) to (3) is to ensure that a taxpayer's RBA is adjusted if an amendment of an assessment results in a refund of a previously assessed amount.
However, subsection 155-75(4) provides that section 155-75 does not apply to the extent that the earlier liability is reinstated, if:
•	a later amendment of an assessment is made, and
•	all or some of the earlier liability in relation to a particular is reinstated.
That is, all or part of the earlier credit to the taxpayer's RBA must be reversed.
 
