6. Amendments during the period of review
6.1 Making an amendment during the period of review
Subsection 155-35(1) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the Commissioner may amend an assessment within the period of review for the assessment.
6.2 What is the period of review?
Subsection 155-35(2) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the period of review for an assessment:
•	starts on the day the Commissioner gives you a notice of assessment, and
•	ends 4 years and one day later.
 Example 14: Period of review for GST return
Jarrah runs a jewellery store and is registered for GST. Jarrah uses quarterly tax periods.
Jarrah lodges their GST return for the tax period ending 30 September 2023 on 14 October 2023 (that is, 2 weeks early).
The Commissioner is deemed to have issued a notice of assessment on 14 October 2023.
The period of review starts on 14 October 2023 and ends on 15 October 2027.
The period of review starts when the Commissioner ‘first gives notice of the assessment’. It is therefore always calculated by reference to when the original assessment was made. The date a notice that any amended assessment is issued is not relevant.
The period of review can be extended under subsections 155-35(3) or (4).
 
6.3 Extending the period of review
Under subsections 155-35(3) and (4) of Schedule 1 to the TAA respectively, there are 2 ways to extend the period of review:
•	a Federal Court order
•	by consent.
Federal court orders
Subsection 155-35(3) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the Federal Court may order an extension of the period of review if:
•	the Commissioner has started to examine your affairs in relation to an assessment
•	the Commissioner has not completed the examination within the period of review
•	the Commissioner (during the period of review) applies to the Federal Court for an order extending the period
•	the Court is satisfied that it was not reasonably practicable, or it was inappropriate, for the Commissioner to complete the examination within the period of review, because of
-	any action taken by you, or
-	any failure by you to take action that it would have been reasonable for you to take.
The term examination is not defined and therefore has its ordinary meaning of ‘the act of examining; inspection; inquiry; investigation’. It is a very broad concept.
The phrase ‘in relation to’ and the word ‘affairs’ are also not defined and therefore take their meaning.
Therefore, the ability to extend the period of review covers not only audits, but also includes any examinations of an entity's affairs including reviews, investigations or enquiries.
Consent
Subsection 155-35(4) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that you may (by written notice given to the Commissioner) consent to the extension of the period of review if the Commissioner:
•	has started to examine your affairs in relation to an assessment
•	has not completed the examination within the period of review for the assessment, and
•	during the period of review, asks you to consent to extending the period of review.
More than one extension allowed
Subsection 155-35(5) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that an order may be made (under subsection 155-35(3)) more than once.
Similarly, consent may be given (under subsection 155-345(4)) more than once.
 Example 15: Consent and more than one extension
Devon and Chen are partners in a GST registered partnership that has complex tax affairs.
The Commissioner is still investigating an activity statement of the partnership that was lodged almost 4 years ago.
The Commissioner has:
•	been reviewing several different entries in the partnership's activity statement
•	been making several enquiries about the partnership's affairs
•	made several requests for information which is taking the partnership some time to complete.
The Commissioner becomes aware that it is unlikely the examination of the partnership's affairs will be completed before the end of the period of review.
The Commissioner contacts Devon and Chen and requests a 3-month extension to the period of review. After consulting with their tax advisers, Devon and Chen agree to the extension. This consent will extend the 4-year period of review by 3 months.
If the Commissioner has not completed the examination of the partnership's affairs within this extended period, they may:
•	request a further extension of time from Devon and Chen, or
•	apply to the Federal Court for an order for an extension of time.
