8. Amending amended assessments
8.1 Time limit for amending amended assessments
Section 155-65 of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the Commissioner cannot amend an amended assessment (under section 155-35) if the period of review for the assessment has ended.
There are 2 exceptions to this rule:
•	The Commissioner can amend an amended assessment at any time under sections 155-45 to 155-60.
•	Section 155-70 (refreshed periods of review) overrides section 155-65.
8.2 Refreshed period of review
Subsection 155-70(1) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that section 155-70 applies if the Commissioner has made one (or more) amendments of an assessment of yours (under section 155-35) about a particular.
Subsection 155-70(2) provides that the Commissioner may amend the amended assessment after the end of the period of review for the assessment, if it is:
•	made before the end of 4 years starting on the day after the notice of amended assessment was given to the taxpayer
•	about a particular that was amended during the original period of review, and
•	in respect of a particular that has not already been previously refreshed.
The first bullet point establishes the start date for the refreshed period of review to be when the assessment was last amended during the original period of review.
The second bullet point limits amendments during the refreshed period to a particular that was amended during the original period of review.
The third bullet point permits only one amendment by the Commissioner or, on application, by the taxpayer.
 
Meaning of particular
A ‘particular’ is not defined and therefore takes its ordinary meaning of ‘an individual or distinct part, as an item of a list or enumeration’.
Accordingly, a particular is an increase (or decrease) in the assessable amount such as a single supply or a single acquisition that is subject to GST provided it individually results in a change to the assessable amount. For example, the amount of input tax credit available on an invoice is a ‘particular’.
The time of the refreshed period of review
The refreshed period of review:
•	starts on the day after the day the Commissioner gives an entity a notice of amended assessment for the last amendment made to the particular during the period of review, and
•	ends 4 years after that day.
What amendments can be made during the refreshed period of review
The amendment in the refreshed period of review must be about a particular that was previously amended in the period of review.
An assessment may only be amended once (under section 155-70) in relation to a particular. However, there may be more than one refreshed period of review if different particulars are amended in the period of review.
If a day falls within both the period of review and the refreshed period of review, the rules for the period of review prevail and the amendments are not restricted by the rules in section 155-70.
In other words, there is no limit to the number of times a particular can be amended before the refreshed period of review applies (that is, during the usual period of review).
 
 Example 20: Refreshed period of review
Aroon operates a cafe and is registered for GST.
On 28 October 2019 Aroon lodges a GST return for the tax period ending 30 September 2019.
On 14 October 2023, Aroon applies to the Commissioner for an amendment to claim input tax credits on 2 tax invoices (A and B) of $100 each.
On 25 October 2023, the Commissioner allows the credits on both invoices.
On 30 October 2023 (during the refreshed period of review), the Commissioner reviews Aroon's assessment and disallows the credits in relation to invoice B.
The Commissioner may no longer make any more amendments to the credits in relation to invoice B during the refreshed period of review.
However, the credits in relation to invoice A may still be amended once before 26 October 2027.
 Example 21: One amendment during period of review
Elya is registered for GST and lodges their June 2020 quarterly GST return on 28 July 2020.
The period of review of this deemed notice of assessment expires on 29 July 2024 (assuming the period is not extended by the court or with Elya’s consent).
On 8 December 2022 (date of amended assessment) particular ‘X’ is amended by Elya lodging an amended activity statement.
No further amendments are made to particular ‘X’ before expiry of the period of review on 29 July 2024.
The refreshed period of review for particular ‘X’ ends on 9 December 2026.
 Example 22: Two amendments during period of review
Charu is registered for GST and lodges their June 2017 quarterly GST return on 28 July 2020.
The period of review of this deemed notice of assessment expires on 29 July 2024 (assuming the period is not extended by the court or with Charu’s consent).
On 8 December 2022 (date of amended assessment) particular ‘X’ is amended by Charu lodging an amended activity statement.
On 15 June 2023 (date of amended assessment) particular ‘X’ is amended a second time by Charu lodging an amended activity statement.
No further amendments are made to particular ‘X’ before expiry of the period of review on 29 July 2024.
On 10 January 2024 the Commissioner issues a notice of amended assessment to Charu increasing the input tax credits on particular ‘Y’.
The refreshed period of review for particular ‘X’ ends on 16 June 2027.
The refreshed period of review for particular ‘Y’ ends on 11 January 2028.
 Example 23: Restriction on amendments during refreshed period of review
Baez is registered for GST and lodges their June 2018 quarterly GST return on 28 July 2018.
The period of review of this deemed notice of assessment expires on 29 July 2022 (assuming the period is not extended by the court or with Baez’s consent).
The Commissioner amends Baez's assessment as requested on the application on 5 September 2020.
On 8 December 2020 (date of amended assessment) particular ‘X’ is amended by Baez lodging an amended activity statement.
On 15 June 2021 (date of amended assessment) particular ‘X’ is amended a second time by Baez lodging an amended activity statement.
No further amendments are made to particular ‘X’ before expiry of the period of review on 29 July 2022.
On 10 January 2022 the Commissioner issues a notice of amended assessment to Baez increasing the input tax credits on particular ‘Y’.
On 30 October 2023, during the refreshed period of review for particular ‘Y’, the Commissioner reviews Baez's June 2018 quarterly GST return assessment and disallows the input tax credits in relation to invoice Y.
The Commissioner may no longer make any more amendments to particular ‘Y’ during the refreshed period of review.
However, the amendment of particular ‘X’ may still be amended once before 16 June 2025.
 
Other issues for refreshed periods of review
The refreshed period of review cannot be extended.
Amendments under sections 155-45, 155-50, 155-55 and 155-60 do not result in a refreshed period of review.
The amendments giving rise to a refreshed period of review can be initiated either by the Commissioner or on application by the entity.
Once an amendment has been made to a particular (under section 155-70) the only rights available to the entity are objection rights under Pt IVC of the TAA.
