9. Validity and review of assessments
9.1 Validity of assessments
Section 155-85 of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the validity of any assessment is not affected by non-compliance with the provisions of the TAA or any other taxation law (that is, laws outside the TAA laws that are specifically about assessments).
Therefore, procedural irregularities in the Commissioner's process of assessing a taxpayer under a taxation law (such as the GST Act) do not affect the validity of an assessment.
The purpose of section 155-85 is to prevent challenges against procedural irregularities in the Commissioner’s process of assessing a taxpayer. However (as provided by section 155-90), a taxpayer can still challenge the assessment under the review and appeal provisions in Part IVC of the TAA.
This indicates that the purpose of section 155-85 (together with section 155-90) is to shepherd challenges against assessments through Part IVC of the TAA.
This ensures that challenges are:
•	only directed towards substantive tax liability issues
•	prevented against assessments in non-Part IVC proceedings (such as under the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1997).
9.2 Review of assessments
Section 155-90 of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that you may object (in the manner set out in Part IVC of the TAA) against an assessment of yours if you are dissatisfied with it.
Assessments are reviewable taxation decisions under Part IVC of the TAA.
Section 14ZV limits any objections to the particular or particulars that have been amended.
 
Time limit to make an objection
Paragraph 14ZW(1)(bg) of the TAA provides that a taxation objection (against an indirect tax assessment) must be made within 4 years and one day from the day on which the Commissioner first gives an assessment notice.
Subsection 14ZW(1B) provides that an objection against an amended assessment must be made within the later of:
•	4 years of when the notice of assessment was issued, or
•	60 days after the notice of amended assessment was given.
However, in all cases, the Commissioner has the discretion (under section 14ZX) to grant an extension of time.
How to make the objection
Section 14ZU of the TAA provides that the objection against an assessment (or amended assessment) must:
•	be in writing
•	set out fully and in detail the grounds of objection.
The Commissioner’s obligations
Under section 14ZY of the TAA, the Commissioner is required to decide whether to:
•	allow an objection wholly
•	allow it in part
•	disallow it.
Section 14ZYA provides that the taxpayer can require the Commissioner to make an objection decision if the Commissioner does not do so within 60 days of the objection being lodged.
Further taxpayer appeal rights
Under section 14ZZ of the TAA, a taxpayer who is dissatisfied with the Commissioner's decision on the objection may apply to the Administrative Appeals Tribunal or appeal to the Federal Court against the decision.
