Course description
This course is designed to provide you with an understanding of what, why and when to have a Lodgment, payment and debt conversation with a taxpayer.
•	Duration: 1 hour
•	Proficiency level: Foundation
•	Audience: all ATO staff
•	Prerequisites: none 
Learning outcomes
At the end of this product, you will be able to:
•	explain the importance and benefits of lodgment, payment and debt conversations
•	list the knowledge requirements, identify when and how to have a lodgment, payment and debt conversation
•	understand how effective conversations help the taxpayer and the ATO.
1. What is a Lodgment, payment and debt conversation?
Every interaction with a taxpayer is an opportunity to have a lodgment, payment, and debt (LPD) conversation to promote willing voluntary compliance for all obligations, now and into the future. 
In an LPD conversation, we can use what we know about a taxpayer’s circumstances to tailor our advice and assist them to meet their obligations. 
The ATO follows the Organisational for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) framework to assess a taxpayer’s level of compliance, based on the following:
•	correctly registering in the system 
•	lodging on time 
•	reporting complete and accurate information 
•	paying on time.
We can assist taxpayers by providing information on actions that they can take which enable them to better manage their circumstances. When talking to taxpayers, we should bring any outstanding lodgment and debt obligations to their attention and focus on obtaining payment in full, if appropriate, or a commitment to take action in the shortest timeframe possible. We can provide taxpayers with information about what actions the ATO may take if they choose not to bring their obligations up to date or engage with us. This ensures they have all information relevant to their circumstances and can make an informed decision. 

2. Why lodgment, payment and debt conversations are important
When taxpayers interact with us, they believe they are interacting with ‘one ATO’, so it is important that we adopt this mindset in our conversations. Using these conversations to promote willing on-time participation, helps the ATO to manage lodgment, payment and debt risks, to reduce the collectable debt book and improve the taxpayer and staff experience. It also supports the optimal outcome of debt prevention.
3. The benefits of having a lodgment, payment and debt conversation
Having appropriate LPD conversations earlier with taxpayers means we can now take a whole of ATO approach to the collection of liabilities, while providing support to make it easier for them to lodge and pay on time and avoid penalties or collection action.
The idea of LPD conversations is to build a standard approach that staff in any area can use in conversations with taxpayers or their representatives.
Benefits for the taxpayer include:
•	understanding the tools and resources are available to make it easier to lodge and pay on time and in full
•	awareness of their current position and helping taxpayers take control to meet their obligations and manage their payments
•	understanding how and when we will engage with taxpayers and the actions we may take, including firmer and stronger action if appropriate
•	awareness of how and when to seek help if a taxpayer is facing difficulty, including self-service options
•	helping taxpayers avoid costly interest charges by familiarising them with the interest accrued when debts are not paid in full and on time.
Benefits for staff include:
•	empowering you to effectively use your knowledge of taxpayers to provide the right information, the right way and at the right time
•	addressing issues and supporting taxpayers in a timely manner
•	recognising some taxpayers may need additional support and responding to them as appropriate.
Benefits for the ATO include:
•	strengthening community confidence in the ATO implementing a ‘one ATO’ approach
•	improving payment and lodgment on time, outstanding obligations and associated risks, including the collection of liabilities raised as the result of case work
•	reducing the potential double handling of taxpayers
•	meeting our performance commitments to the government.
4. Knowledge required to have a lodgment, payment and debt conversation
The following key pieces of knowledge will assist you to have effective LPD conversations when engaging with taxpayers.
Payment Thinking
Payment Thinking is about having evolving conversations with taxpayers about lodgments and payments, with the aim of preventing debt from occurring in the first place.
It focuses on staff recognising risks to lodgment and payment on time at every stage of the  tax and superannuation lifecycle and taking action. It is also about having taxpayers meet their obligations by making it easier to lodge and pay on time and ensuring they are aware of the consequences of not lodging and paying on time.
Applying a Payment Thinking mindset helps encourage lodgment and payment at the earliest possible stage in the taxation process to reduce or completely avoid any financial risks. It helps build your confidence to address potential or actual lodgment and payment issues and provides you with the skill to commit taxpayers to lodge and pay on time.
Additional resources to build your Payment Thinking - capability are available on myATO.
Know the taxpayer
Understanding the taxpayers' situation helps you to tailor your conversation and better engage the taxpayer. You should use information held in ATO systems to 'Know your taxpayer' and understand their lodgments, accounts and compliance behaviour.
The Enterprise Client Profile (ECP) can help you profile the taxpayer, provide a whole of client view and personalise the conversation you have. ECP draws information from multiple systems into one place, so that you can get to know a taxpayer’s situation quickly and efficiently - behaviours, lodgment and debt position, financial resilience index scores and other information to shape your conversation. Gathering information from systems is an important part of getting to know your client - talking and listening to your client will provide further information.
Further learning is available if you need more detail on ECP.
Additional resources to build your Knows our clients - core knowledge are available in the ATO capability framework.
Know our business
Knowing the core business of the ATO and key attributes is essential to helping support it to “encourage willing and accurate participation in the tax and super system to ensure the economic and social wellbeing of Australia”. (Managing the tax and super system)
We build trust and confidence by working closely with taxpayers to understand their circumstances, so we can provide the support most suited to them. We take a differentiated approach with a focus on timely engagement, recognising the importance of helping taxpayers to stay connected with the tax and superannuation systems.
We continuously reflect and act on feedback from the community and our staff, making sure that our approach is appropriate and effective.
Additional resources to build your Knows our business - core knowledge are available in the ATO capability framework.
Know our processes
To ensure consistent service, it is important to know which processes to follow and where to find them. Many areas use the Enterprise Knowledge Management (EKM) system however, you must always refer to specific business procedures for your area.
Actions to consider for the purpose of an LPD conversation are:
•	obtaining a commitment to lodge, and
•	obtaining payment in full or where that is not possible a payment plan for the shortest possible timeframe, if appropriate, 
•	Providing legal warning where appropriate, and
•	incorporating future liabilities and obligations into our conversations.
Informing taxpayers (and their representatives) about the online self-service options available is also encouraged. There are a variety of actions taxpayers and their representatives can complete online without the need to contact us further. This allows taxpayers to take control of their accounts and understand what options are best suited to their individual circumstances.
Additional resources to build your Knows our business - core knowledge are available in the ATO capability framework.
Obtaining a commitment to lodge
If you identify the taxpayer has lodgments coming up to their due date or are overdue, including this as part of your conversation allows for lodgment on-time to be promoted through natural conversations.
This provides the ATO with a fuller picture of the taxpayer’s situation, it also provides the taxpayer with certainty that can be used to plan and take control of their circumstances.
Obtaining payment in full or where that is not possible a payment plan, if appropriate
Payment in full should always be discussed as an option before setting up a payment plan.
Discussing payment in full today can seem difficult, but setting this expectation with the taxpayer is crucial. Tax is not optional - all taxpayers have an obligation to pay in full and on time. By driving payment today, we work towards building good payment habits and making tax obligations a priority for all taxpayers. This is not only good for Australia, but for the taxpayer as well. The longer it takes a taxpayer to pay, the more it will cost them through interest charges and potential penalties. Interest charges and penalties will not be remitted except in exceptional circumstances.
Our payment conversations should be approached by understanding: 
•	why payment was not made by the due date 
•	when the total debt will be paid 
•	what the taxpayer is doing to ensure future obligations will be lodged and paid on time
•	the impact General Interest Charge (GIC) will have and educating taxpayers on how to avoid paying GIC.
•	possible further actions the ATO may take and ensuring taxpayers are aware of the consequences of not engaging
Having tailored, outcome focused conversations, guiding taypayers towards lodging and paying on time, is essential to building good payment habits and preventing debt from escalating. 
When payment plans are granted, instalments do not need to be equal across the life of the plan. Refer to your business area procedures for further instructions when negotiating payment plans.


5. When to have a Lodgment Payment and Debt conversation
Every time you engage with a taxpayer or their representative, there is an opportunity for you to have an LPD conversation. For example, when there is an appropriate opportunity, you should discuss:
•	the taxpayers' obligations (current, due shortly and overdue)
•	how they can find related information on ato.gov.au
•	how to self-serve using Online Services (individual, agent or business) or other assistance available 
•	payment in full and on time or potential payment plans to pay the debt in the shortest possible time
•	legal warnings where appropriate.
Examples
The taxpayers' obligations (current, due shortly and overdue)
“While I’ve got you, I noticed some of your lodgments are overdue, let’s discuss how we can bring them up to date”
“While I’ve got you, I noticed some of your client’s lodgments are overdue. Can you please confirm your plan to get them up to date?”
“Depending on the outcome of this case you may have an amount owing, if that is the case have you given any thought as to do how you will pay this on time?”
Payment in full and on time or potential payment plans
“Are you aware that there is an amount outstanding on your account?  Can you pay it in full today?”
“Are you aware that your client also has an amount outstanding on their account?  Can they pay it in full today?”
“If you aren’t able to pay the full amount now will you be able to pay it in full in 30 days?”
“To negotiate a payment plan I will need to collect some financial information from you. This ensures we negotiate a sustainable plan that will have you debt free as soon as possible and reduce the interest you will need to pay”.
“You should be aware that the ATO make take further action to recover the debt if you don’t engage with us.”
How to self-serve using Online Services (individual, agent or business)
“Do you have access to ATO Online Services? You can use this to lodge your activity statements and keep track of your obligations, would you like more information?”
“Did you know you can enter a payment plan through our self-help options? I can show you how…”

 
6. Managing conversations
When talking to taxpayers about lodgment and payment, there are some important skills you can utilise to keep the conversation on track towards the desired outcome. This section provides a summary of some of those skills.
Negotiation skills and questioning techniques
You can employ negotiation skills and questioning techniques to assist in your payment conversations. What does this look like?
Be organised and prepared
Taxpayers are more likely to take action or engage with us when we tailor our conversation to their individual circumstances. If they can see that the payment options offered clearly reflect their specific circumstances the conversation will be more meaningful to them.
Use a variety of questions
Ask questions as necessary to understand the taxpayers's needs and circumstances.   
Listen to the taxpayer’s answers; both what they say and how they say it. Respond in a way that reinforces that you have heard and understood them.
Define the outcome
Define the outcome you are working towards, and clearly communicate that to the taxpayer. This might be emphasising the importance of paying in full, or paying in the shortest possible timeframe if the taxpayer cannot achieve this.  When there is transparency in your process and you are both in alignment on the outcome you’re trying to achieve then the taxpayer is more likely to be satisfied with the final outcome. 
Taking back control of the conversation – assertiveness
What is assertive communication?  
Assertive communication is exercising your right to express your ideas, feelings and needs, while respecting other people's right to do the same.   
Why is it important to be assertive?  
How do you balance active listening with maintaining control of your payment conversations?  
It’s important to understand that if the taxpayers’s wants or needs don’t align with the ATO’s payment posture, then you will need to use assertive communication to manage the conversation and the outcome. Taxpayers are more likely to collaborate when you communicate assertively.
Points to draw out in the conversation  
Assertive  
•	voice is well modulated  
•	acknowledging the needs of others  
•	stating your own feelings  
•	using direct statements  
•	being relaxed.  
“Yes, I can see that you were granted a longer payment plan previously. However, the ATO’s expectation is that debts are paid on time and in full. We will need to review your capacity to pay to determine the shortest possible timeframe that you can pay.”
Engaging with angry or abusive taxpayers  
The following steps can assist you to manage an angry taxpayer:  
•	listen and acknowledge their concerns without being defensive,  
•	respond to their situation rather than reacting to their tone,  
•	remain calm and patient, and  
•	use positive and non-judgmental language, like ‘Let me help you find a solution.’
If the taxpayer is acting inappropriately you should:
•	give the caller an opportunity to change their behaviour 
•	suggest that the taxpayer call back when they are calmer
•	if the taxpayer’s behaviour does not change, tell them that you find their behaviour unacceptable 
•	if necessary, tell the taxpayer that you will terminate the call if the behaviour continues
•	if the behaviour continues, terminate the call, immediately discuss the incident with your team leader.
Further information
•	Managing inappropriate client behaviour framework
•	Telephony Guide
Navigating interactions where the taxpayer is emotional
While it’s important to emphasise the ATO’s posture shift regarding payment posture, there may be some taxpayers struggling financially, mentally, or both. In this section we will look at how you can navigate interactions where the taxpayer is emotional.   
Remember: You are not expected to take on any role that should be handled by a qualified professional, such as a psychologist or Financial Advisor. You should be careful not to inadvertently slip into a counsellor role or provide financial advice.  If you have any concerns, speak to your team leader.
Using positive language  
Using positive language helps set the tone of an interaction and move it towards a desirable outcome. Talk about what can be done rather than what can’t and the positive behaviour or action your taxpayer needs to take.  
•	Make taxpayers the focus of your attention as it shows that you understand and are ready to assist with their query or concern.  
•	Put yourself in their position. You will get a better understanding of the issue from the taxpayer’s viewpoint.  
•	Taxpayers want to feel that they are listened to and treated as an individual.  
•	Give yourself time to reflect on what the taxpayer has said before responding. Pausing will allow you time to think of a good response. 
•	Positive language. This can help you to stay more in control of the conversation and improve taxpayer engagement.

7. Scenarios
Here is an opportunity for you to practise what you have learnt in this training.
Scenario 1 – Individual unexpected Capital Gain
Mani sold a capital asset which was not exempt from capital gains tax, which has resulted in a large capital gain. Mani included the capital gain in the tax return, however was surprised by the resulting debit assessment as they normally receive a refund. Mani has called the ATO to check if this is correct.
After passing Proof Of Record Ownership (PORO), you access Mani’s records and review the information available. You explain to Mani that unlike salary and wage income, the capital gain did not have tax withheld. As a result, when the income tax return was lodged there was an amount owing with no corresponding credit.
Mani only realised they had to include the capital gain while working through the tax return and states they are unable to pay the amount owing in full.
Scenario 1 - question
Is there anything else you should tell Mani?
Scenario 1 - debrief
To provide the best possible taxpayer experience you should have a payment conversation to understand why you cannot pay in full and establish if the taxpayer can pay something today and what is the shortest possible timeframe to pay the debt. Could it be: 
•	within 30 days 
•	within 3 months 
Throughout the interaction, educate the taxpayer on the General Interest Charge (GIC) implications if they are not able to pay in full today, or in a shorter period of time. 
TIP: Advising the taxpayer of the current GIC rate can assist in negotiating payment sooner.  
Make sure Mani knows the due date, direct Mani to the ‘How to Pay’ page, discuss payment options and how Mani can self-serve. You should also prompt Mani to consider what steps they might take if they were to have another capital gain.
You end the call by confirming that Mani is aware of when the debt is due and the consequences of not complying. You also provide information on where to find the ‘How to pay’ page on the ATO website and how to self-serve if a payment plan is required. You advise Mani that if they anticipate another capital gain in the future, they can use the information on the website to estimate what the tax obligation might be. You also suggest to Mani, in the event of another capital gain, to consider putting aside some money or making a pre-payment to their Income Tax account as a planning tool. Mani agrees this is a good suggestion and one that might be relevant in a couple of years.
The following key topics were covered in this scenario:
•	Handling an unexpected debt
•	Education on how to find information on ATO website
•	Taxpayer education on self-service of a payment plan
•	Payment and Debt conversation
Some of the benefits provided by this approach include:
Taxpayer benefits
•	Understands their obligations and future lodgments
•	Certainty of how to manage & pay obligation
•	Knowledge on how to navigate the ATO website for educational information
•	Able to self-serve providing flexibility and convenience
ATO benefits
•	Increased payments received and reduced debt
•	Improved use of resources when taxpayers use self-service options
•	Build trust and confidence and increased taxpayer satisfaction contributing to voluntary compliance outcomes
Staff benefits
•	Empowering staff to effectively use their knowledge
•	Increased job satisfaction building positive relationships to engage the taxpayer in a timely manner with the correct information
Scenario 2 – Individual managing tax affairs
Terrin has lodged this year’s Income Tax return on time with several deductions but has some previous lodgments outstanding.
You are actioning a case, checking the deductions claimed by Terrin. The initial assessment has an amount owing and if the deductions are disallowed, the debt will increase. The debt is due in two months’ time, you note that two prior years’ returns have not been lodged.
You have a conversation with Terrin about the case explaining the reason for it being selected and the anticipated timelines.
Scenario 2 - question
Do you think that you should mention anything else?
Scenario 2 - debrief
To provide the best possible taxpayer experience you can confirm that Terrin is aware of the outstanding lodgments and prompt them to consider appropriate actions.
You also raise the issue of the outstanding lodgments and the current debit assessment. You explain to Terrin that lodging the outstanding tax returns will provide certainty towards their overall tax position, allowing them to take control managing their affairs. You also advise that when the case is finalised, the debt owing may or may not change and confirm Terrin is aware of the due date and is putting funds aside to be able to pay the debt.
Terrin comments they were juggling taking care of a young child while studying and hadn’t worked full-time so had forgotten to lodge the outstanding returns. You remind Terrin that the employment information during those years may be available in myTax as prefilled data.
Terrin lodges the two outstanding tax returns and finds that they result in refunds which reduces the overall debt. After reviewing Terrin’s case, you determine that some deductions will be disallowed and explain this to Terrin who advises that they will use this knowledge to correctly claim future deductions. These changes increase Terrin’s debit assessment for that year.
After the initial conversation with you about payment, Terrin had started putting aside some money every week for the expected debit assessment. With this planning and the impact of the refunds from the previously overdue lodgments Terrin is now able to pay in full, by the due date.
The following key topics were covered in this scenario:
•	Debit assessment
•	Use of myTax & prefill
•	Outstanding lodgment
•	Deductions
•	Lodgment, Payment and Debt conversation through case
Some of the benefits provided by this approach include:
Taxpayer benefits
•	Records are up to date, relief at addressing outstanding issues and certainty
•	Educated, understands current position and has knowledge to use in the future
•	As prompted, planned and took action and will be able to meet obligations on time
•	Able to self-serve, providing flexibility and convenience
ATO benefits
•	Improve payment on time
•	Obtain outstanding lodgments
•	Improving taxpayer satisfaction and engagement contributing to voluntary compliance outcomes
Staff benefits
•	Empowering staff to effectively use their knowledge to assist the taxpayer and address issues
•	Reduced future workflow by address outstanding lodgments
•	Positive feedback and job satisfaction from positive taxpayer engagement
Scenario 3 – Individual objection and amendment
Francis received a default assessment with associated penalties on their Income Tax account. They lodge an objection and an amendment form claiming some large deductions.
You contact Francis to introduce yourself as the case resolver as required by step 2.7 of the ISB procedures (engaging with the taxpayer and understanding their circumstances) During this discussion you provide an overview of Francis’ tax position i.e. any known outstanding lodgments / debts on their account, and why it’s important for them to take action and also ask questions to assist you in making the objection decision, for example, how the deductions relate to the income earning activities Francis advises that a letter demanding payment has been received and mentions that a friend suggested an objection be lodged to claim large deductions which would remove Francis’ debt.
Scenario 3 - question
How would you respond to Francis?
Scenario 3 - debrief
Francis should be advised that if the deductions are related to the income earned, they will be processed. If they’re not, Francis should think about how the debt will be paid and check they understand their obligations to lodge and pay on time.
You explain that the advice they received from their friend is not entirely correct. If the deductions cannot be substantiated, the objection would not be allowed, and the amount owing would still need to be paid and this should be considered. You discuss what led to the default assessment, whether there might be any grounds for remission and use your understanding of the taxpayer’s circumstances to ascertain their eligibility for remission. To help Francis understand their lodgment and payment obligations, you provide them with the ‘Paying your tax liability’ fact sheet, and any other relevant tools and guide them to appropriate information on the ATO website.
After reviewing the additional information provided regarding their deductions, you advise them that the objection has been allowed in part, and that they were eligible for remission of the penalties imposed as a result of the information provided. You arrange to send Francis a Notice of Decision and have a payment discussion to establish if they can pay in full today, otherwise if appropriate offer to help them set up a payment plan for the debt or direct them to the online self-service options. Francis is reluctant to commit to paying the debt in full or entering into a payment plan. You let Francis know the ATO may take further debt recovery action if the debt is not addressed including garnisheeing her wages or bank account or legal action. Francis seems surprised but thanks you for the information and confirms she will not let that happen and will be entering a payment plan online. 
The following key topics were covered in this scenario:
•	Handling an unexpected debt and expectations of payment on time
•	Education on how to find information on the ATO website
•	Taxpayer education on self-service of a payment plan
•	Education on further possibly recovery action
•	Payment and Debt conversation Some of the benefits provided by this approach include:
Taxpayer benefits
•	Understands and can manage an unexpected debt
•	Understands their obligations and future lodgments
•	Certainty of how to manage and pay their obligation
•	Educated on how to navigate the website for educational information
•	Able to self-service giving the taxpayer flexibility and convenience
ATO benefits
•	Increased payments received and reduced debt
