•	Improved use of resources when taxpayers use self-service options
•	Taxpayer understanding contributes to future voluntary compliance outcomes
Staff benefits
•	Empowering staff to effectively use their knowledge
•	Increased job satisfaction building positive relationships to engage the taxpayer
Scenario 4 – Small Business overdue tax obligations
Jaya owns a flower and gift shop that has been operating for about a year and is still working out the best way to manage everything. You recently called Jaya about some outstanding activity statements. Because of the demands of the shop, Jaya did not have time to talk at the time. Jaya has now called back to have a more detailed discussion about their tax obligations.
Jaya knows that there are some overdue activity statement lodgments and is concerned that they won’t be able to pay the amounts owing when the lodgments are up to date. Jaya also tells you that they have found it challenging to bring the records up to date so they can fully understand the current position.
You remind Jaya of the obligation to lodge and pay on time and the potential penalties and interest charges that may apply if this does not happen. You direct Jaya to the Online services for business.
Scenario 4 - question
Do you think that you could have included anything else in this discussion?
Scenario 4 - debrief
To provide the best possible taxpayer experience you should find out when Jaya intends to lodge and suggests Jaya looks at resources available to help manage their business records.
You understand that Jaya is new to business and advise that there are many free resources available on the ATO website and Business.gov.au that can assist with record keeping and cash management. Equipped with this information, Jaya can then decide if they want to seek further professional advice from an accountant/bookkeeper.
You advise Jaya that by bringing their lodgments up to date, they will better understand their tax position, and avoid possible penalties, default assessments or legal action. This certainty will enable Jaya to more accurately plan and manage their finances.
Jaya commits to getting the lodgments up to date by an agreed date but is still concerned about their ability to pay. You explain to Jaya that funds they collect on behalf of the government, such as GST as PAYG, should not form part of their cash flow and need to be set aside so they can be remitted when due. Provided Jaya can lodge and pay on time going forward, the ATO may allow a payment plan to pay off the debt resulting from the late lodgments.  Jaya would need to pay the debt in the shortest possible time, based on income and expenses, which would also be best for Jaya’s business as it would reduce the amount of interest they would be required to pay. You direct Jaya to the information on the ATO website to find out more at a convenient time and explore the self-service options.
Jaya thanks you for your assistance and states that they will be more comfortable managing their affairs in future and accessing available resources. Jaya comments that dealing with the ATO wasn’t as ‘stressful as I expected’.
The following key topics were covered in this scenario:
•	Handling a taxpayer new to small business
•	Education on online services & self help
•	Education on cash flow and record management
•	Lodgment and Payment conversation
Some of the benefits provided by this approach include:
Taxpayer benefits
•	Records are up to date and understands the importance of lodging on time
•	Understands current position and has more certainty
•	Knowledge of resources and tools to track and manage their circumstances
•	Able to self-serve, providing flexibility and convenience
ATO benefits
•	Improved lodgment and payment on time
•	Obtain outstanding lodgments
