2.	Course description
This course provides you with information on the Information Notices and Garnishee Online Tool (INGOT).
Duration	30 mins

Proficiency Level	Foundation

Audience	All ATO staff

Prerequisites	There are no prerequisites for this course.
Learning outcomes
After the completion of this course, you will be able to:
•	Describe what the INGOT system is
•	Describe the main INGOT functions
•	Demonstrate how to launch and navigate the INGOT system
•	Identify your INGOT system access role(s).

 
1.	Introduction
Dealing with electronic requests
This video provides an overview of the Electronic Exchange and INGOT and some high-level changes that you need to be aware of as a result of moving to an electronic exchange for these notices.
Transcript of INGOT Overview video
The way the ATO sends statutory information and garnishee notices to financial institutions and receives responses is changing. Today we are introducing you to the Information Notices and Garnishees Notices Online Tool or INGOT. INGOT is designed for the ATO to generate and issue certain electronic notices to financial institutions and receive their responses. It was developed based on an agreed electronic exchange to securely transmit information.
Why do you need to learn about INGOT? 
By using INGOT and the electronic exchange it changes: 
•	the way you prepare a notice
•	the number of notices you may need to issue; and 
•	when you issue those notices. 
Using this technology correctly, we will: 
•	streamline our processes 
•	reduce costs for the ATO and financial institutions
•	improve the way government agencies exchange information with financial institutions; and 
•	reduce our environmental footprint. 
With INGOT, getting responses is much faster. 
For manual information gathering notices it can take between 30 to 90 days; electronically, usually just three to 10 days, but there is a change to the approach for information notices. It’s been agreed that when we issue information notices electronically, we will stage our requests. Our first request will primarily be to identify the customer at the bank. For these requests the bank will usually respond within three days. Subsequent requests for more specific detail will be issued using information from an electronic response and the bank will respond to these within 10 days in most instances. So, in some types of activity, you may issue more notices, but the responses will rocket back so you can keep progressing.
The financial institution will usually respond to a garnishee notice within 3 days, noting that the garnishee is likely actioned prior to the date the response is received. 
So, let’s look at how it works
Let’s say you have a notice to issue to a financial institution; you check can I issue this electronically. For electronic notices you will open INGOT and create a new notice. INGOT standardised request forms mean no more inconsistencies or ambiguity in your request, and this means a much faster and more accurate response. 
You will submit the request and the notice will move into a batch file ready to be sent to the financial institution. Batching occurs at regular times throughout the day via the bulk data exchange.  This cuts out the delays we experience when sending by post and waiting for paper notices to move through manual bank processes. 
The receiving bank will log onto a secure portal and download batch files from the data exchange directly into their system. Some requests will be processed automatically with no human intervention. Others may require manual intervention in part or in full. This will be dependent on the request type, matching parameters, and the maturity of the receiving financial institution’s system. You will learn more about matching in INGOT training. 
When a response to an information notice or a Standard or Merchant garnishee is received you will be alerted by email. As a minimum the response to an information notice will indicate whether there is a matching customer or customers at the bank. Provide a set of certain customer details for each customer and provide a set of response detail relevant to the type of request. A response to a garnishee notice will indicate whether there is a matching customer at the bank and the outcome of the garnishee. 
Depending on the type of request and your activity you may need to review the response. 
If you receive a response to an information notice, you will be checking that the customer returned is the one you are looking for and considering any further information you need to progress your activity. If you need to obtain further information you will submit a subsequent request. For example, you might need bank account transactions, depending on the financial institution, you can ask for this as transaction detail or as a copy of the bank statement. 
One of the biggest savings of obtaining bank information electronically is getting transaction detail in electronic format as data from certain financial institutions. Why, because at the click of a button it can be easily exported to excel for quick analysis. No more days spent re-keying bank statement information or cross checking and correcting optical character recognition errors. If the financial institution’s system is not yet able to do this, you will receive PDF copies of statements and there are a range of tools available to you to convert these to excel.
So, what can you ask for electronically?
In most instances you can ask for everything you ask for when issuing a manual information gathering notice, the key difference is how and when you ask for it. 
So how did all this come about?
The tax office got together with other government agencies and financial institutions and developed a set of standardised ways that garnishee and information notices would be processed. They agreed on the secure data mechanisms that would be used now and into the future to safeguard information. The bulk data exchange (BDE) will be used until we are ready to move to a whole of government web services solution. 
Now it is a case for each agency and each bank building the systems on their end to enable the exchange. This bit is not so straightforward because it requires an investment from each party to provide a carefully planned testing schedule to make it work. To manage this, separate implementations will be introduced progressively as each financial institution comes on board. 
This process will take some time. While this is happening you must know which institutions exchange electronically and which exchange manually so you can send notices in the correct format. Once a financial institution moves to an electronic exchange, there are very few circumstances in which they will accept manual notices. If a manual notice is required, the INGOT Administrator team manages sending the notice to the financial institution via the ATO’s secure Data Transfer Facility and the financial institution will use the same channel to send the response.    
So that’s the overview of INGOT and the electronic exchange. 
Before we go let’s just recap:
•	The ATO is moving to exchanging information with financial institutions electronically. 
•	We will be doing this with INGOT and the bulk data exchange. 
•	INGOT will be introduced to banks progressively and you need to know which format manual or electronic the bank will accept. 
•	Issuing electronic information gathering notices means you ask for information progressively rather than all at once. 
•	You may issue more information gathering notices, but responses will rocket back to you to keep you progressing. 
•	And finally, now when you need bank account transactions you can ask for that in data format from certain financial institutions, which at the touch of a button can be exported to excel for quick analysis. If the financial institution’s system is not yet able to do this, you will receive PDF copies of statements and there are a range of tools available to you to convert
