Course description
This course describes how to use the Information Notices and Garnishee Online Tool (INGOT) to issue information notices (excluding CAS payment traces) to financial institutions.
Course details
•	Duration: 1 hour
•	Proficiency level: Foundation
•	Audience: INGOT users
•	Prerequisites: Related Courses
-	INGOT introduction
-	INGOT CAS payment trace & info notices
-	INGOT garnishee notice
-	INGOT administrator
-	INGOT reporting
Learning outcomes
At the end of this course, you will be able to:
•	Describe the INGOT information notice process.
•	Understand how to create a new information notice using the INGOT system.
•	Describe how to submit an information notice and / or send the notice for approval using the INGOT system.
•	Understand the response detail provided by a responding financial institution.
•	Outline how to create a new information notice based on a response using the INGOT system.
•	Describe how to close an INGOT notice.
 
1. Introduction
Obtaining information from financial institutions
Manual notices vs electronic notices issued via INGOT
Issuing electronic notices via INGOT impacts how we approach and structure them.
When we issue a manual paper notice we rely on a person at the financial institution assessing our request and making their own decisions around what we need. They provide a response based on this assessment. Manual paper correspondence is slow, so we generally request as much information as possible in the first notice to minimise further correspondence.
Using INGOT, a different approach is required as the identification of customers will generally be automated by the financial institution.
To accommodate this, our initial notice will focus on identifying the correct customer(s) at the financial institution. Once this has been done, you can submit subsequent notices to obtain any further information and/or documents required for your case activities, noting each notice is regarding a single customer.
Also, as the notice is being sent directly to the financial institution via INGOT, there is no need to provide a covering letter. INGOT does produce a copy of the notice for printing if required.
 
2. How the process works
Initial notice
In your initial notice you will provide the financial institution with the known demographic detail about the entity you are trying to identify.
The financial institution will use these details to conduct a search for potential matches and this process may be automated. Depending on the information provided, one or more matched customers may be returned.
The financial institution uses a set of agreed match types to identify any customers in their database that match the information you have provided in the Subject of enquiry section of your notice. INGOT automatically determines the match type based on that information.
As a general rule you should provide all known demographic details available for the subject of your enquiry. In some cases, you may only have a BSB and account number and need to determine the owner(s) of that account and these details would be all that you include in the notice.
The financial institution will apply the relevant matching process using the information provided in the notice and the result will be one of the following:
•	no match - where the financial institution has not been able to identify any matching customers.
•	a single customer match - where the financial institution has identified one matching customer: or
•	a list of matching customers that the financial institution has identified, noting that some financial institutions may have multiple profiles for a single customer and each profile will be listed.
The response may also include a Weak match count, and this is the number of customers that match some of the information provided but not enough to meet the point threshold to be returned as a matching customer.
The search results are compiled into a response, and you can view this in INGOT.
You will then review the response against the initial notice.
•	Has the response identified the entity you are enquiring about? If not, and you have evidence that the entity holds accounts at the financial institution, assess what other information is held that could be included in a new notice, for example, only providing a BSB and account number.
•	Is there any more information that is required for this case? If so, you will need to submit a subsequent INGOT notice which must be created from within the response.
Using a Customer Reference Number
A Customer Reference Number (CRN) is the unique client identifier the financial institution uses for their customer.
For most initial information notices, you will not know the CRN.
This means you must complete an initial notice to obtain the customer details, including the CRN.
Once you have obtained a matching customer, you can request any additional information or documents that are required for the purposes of your investigation. 
 
 
