5. Response received
Receiving a response
A response will be generally received between 3 to 10 business days, depending on the nature of the request.
The detail and the format that the financial institution can provide is governed by their systems and how they store information. Some information will readily be accessible from one or more databases and can be returned in an electronic data format.
Some information may be stored in hard copy and may need to be obtained from off-site stores, scanned, and provided as an electronic image of the paper document. How the information is stored at the financial institution may also impact on how quickly the response can be returned.
When a response is received from the financial institution you will be notified via email.
The email is automatically generated by INGOT and will include the INGOT ID that the response relates to.
Open INGOT and locate the request either by:
•	opening the My Requests view 
o	Select My Requests from the yellow navigation bar. 
o	Select the View link on the appropriate request.
•	using the Search function. 
o	Select Search from the yellow navigation bar. 
o	Enter the INGOT ID in the search field. 
o	In the search results, select the View link.
Note: Responses in INGOT are attached to the original notice. When you open a notice in ‘Response Received’ status you will be navigated directly to the information response page. Use the View Original Request button to return to the original notice if necessary.
 
Information Response screen
 
The Information Response screen shows summary information about the response. In this case we are looking at information about Joe Bloggs. More information can be found in the major sections of the screen. (For the purposes of this training, the customer ‘Joe Bloggs’ is a fictitious individual.)
 
View Original Request button
 
This button will take you to the notice so that you can cross check the response with what was requested from the financial institution.
Response Messages section
 
This section shows any response messages received from the financial institution. 
Response messages can be system generated or specific financial institution messages, for example: weak match count (the number of customers that match some details but not enough to create a positive match), no match, or ‘Please resubmit using CRN xxx’.
Response Customers section
 
This section provides a list of all customers of the financial institution that have been returned in the response.
The customers are potential matches for the subject of enquiry in your notice. If multiple customers are returned, you should review the detail for each one to determine which are in fact your subject of enquiry and proceed to request further detail regarding those customers.
 Attention 
Some financial institutions may have multiple profiles for a single customer. Each profile will have a separate CRN and be returned as a separate customer.
Use the View link to access the details for each customer in the Response Customer section.
 
Response Customer Details screen 
 
We will now look at ‘Joe Bloggs’ details by selecting the View button next to his name on the Response Customers section on the Information Response screen.
Always use the Back button on the screen (when it is available) instead of the Back button in the browser. This will prevent data loss and errors.
The Org. Name fields are used when the customer is a non-individual.
Use this Address and Contact Details to crosscheck address and contact details with the details you currently have. Record differences for your case as appropriate.
If the account details are provided as data, the ‘Accounts’ section will include a list of all accounts linked to the matched customer that were active during your period of enquiry. Alternately, the financial institution may provide this information in a file attached to the response. The attachment will be provided at the Customer level, and you can view this by selecting the Documents button.
A response to a question or a requested document may be provided by the responding financial institution at either the Customer level or the Account level. You can access the documents by selecting the Documents button and the responses to questions by selecting the Custom questions button. 
What does this mean? You will find the response either in the Response Customer Details view or in the Response Account Details view. We will talk about the Response Account details screen later.
Ensure that you fully consider the opening and closing dates for each account returned and that the requests you make for information and documents relate to periods in which the account was active.
Response Account Details screen
 
If the account details are provided as data and appear in the ‘Accounts’ section. Selecting the View link will open a detailed view of the account.
Note, if the account details are provided in a file attached to the response, you will need to open the attachment to view the information.
This screen provides further details about Joe’s accounts.
Always use the Back button on the screen (when it is available) instead of the Back button in the browser. This will prevent data loss and errors.
Response Account Details section
 
The Response Account Details section, or a file attached to the response, provides you with general account summary detail. 
The customer’s role in relation to the account is also available in this section.
A status of ‘Enabled’ means that it is an active account with the financial institution.
A status of ‘Disabled’ means it is no longer active. Accounts with a ‘Disabled ‘status will always have a closed date provided.

Balance Details and Document Details sections 
The Balance Details section will provide the balance type, balance date and the balance itself.
The Document Details section provides you with any documents that have been returned at the account level. The documents will generally be returned as a scanned image file of the document (in .tif or .pdf format) which can be opened by selecting on the link to the file. Where a document is not available, the financial institution may include an explanation in the Other information column or in an attached file.
 Attention 
