Retaining the refund – why do we retain?
Taxpayers have an expectation to receive a prompt payment 
or refund upon lodging their tax obligations. 
Our responsibility is to safeguard the tax system and to find a 
balance between preserving the integrity of the tax system 
and mitigating opportunities for fraudulent refunds. 
Section 8AAZLGA of the Taxation Administration Act 1953 
allows the Commissioner to retain, in certain circumstances, a 
running balance account (RBA) surplus/credit, that the ATO 
would otherwise have to refund the taxpayer pursuant to 
s8AAZLF. 
Introduction of section 
8AAZLGA
This legislation was introduced as a 
result of a decision made in 
Commissioner of Taxation v Multiflex
Pty Ltd [2011] FCAFC 142 (Multiflex).
Importance of section 
8AAZLGA
Commissioners discretion provided in 
s8AALZGA enables us to verify 
information provided by taxpayers 
prior to releasing the refund. 
It is empirical that the discretion be 
applied correctly and consistently, as 
failure to do so may allow taxpayers to 
take legal action. 
