Bank statements and loan documents
We request bank statements to address a couple of risks:
• They can be evidence that the client has made payment for the purchases (aka consideration) showing on their GST transaction listing
• Given that our clients are in a refund position, which means that their purchases exceed their sales, a bank statement can show that the 
client generally has the money to fund the business whilst their purchases are higher than their sales 
• Bank statements can also show us if the client has made more sales than what they have reported on their business activity statement 
We generally request loan documents as substantiation for capital purchases such as property, heavy vehicles such as trucks and large 
equipment or machinery. However, we may ask for a loan document to substantiate a client’s ability to fund their enterprise more generally. 
INTERNAL
Creditable importations
The client must hold documentation showing goods have been imported and GST was paid or deferred at the time the goods were entered 
for home consumption. No tax invoice is required.
Goods are entered for home consumption by completing an import declaration and submitting it to the Department of Home Affairs, who 
release the goods when customs duty and GST has been paid.
Accepted documents for Imports:
• Import Declaration N10: provides details of values and charges for goods that are initially entered for home consumption, including 
deferred GST and total payable amount
• Import Declaration N30: provides details of values and charges for goods these goods when they are cleared out of a customs licensed 
warehouse, including deferred GST and total payable amount.
• Official receipts are issued by Home Affairs for payments received, including GST.
