What we do once we receive documents?
Confirm whether the supplier’s ABN listed on the tax invoice is active and they are registered for 
GST.
Confirm whether the supplier’s name matches that on the invoice – may be legal or trading name.
Identify any relationship between the supplier and purchaser – matching associates or 
representatives, contact details, bank details etc.
Check whether supplier has lodged corresponding BAS and whether reported sales (label G1) are 
greater than the invoiced amount.
Check for evidence that supplier is in administration or has an undischarged bankrupt indicator on 
their account.
NOTE: sometimes invoices will contain supplier details that do not match the GST registration (e.g. 
trusts) – if ths occurs, advise your client to obtain an invoice with the correct details.
INTERNAL
Reviewing tax invoices – system checks
A supplier of taxable supplies must be registered, or required to be registered, for GST.
If a supplier is unregistered and:
• Not required to be registered: do not allow the GST credits claimed by the client unless the supplier has registered and backdated their 
registration to take into account the supply.
• Required to be registered: 
–Allow the GST credits claimed by the client
–Educate the supplier of their requirement to be registered
–Escalate supplier details via ATO Intelligence to ensure registration and required lodgments occur.
INTERNAL
Registration status of supplier
A third party check is a the verification step we do to verify the details in a tax invoice by the supplier. The process generally involves 
making contact with the supplier. 
Before conducting a third party check:
• Check to see whether the supplier is a related or associated entity of the client and whether the sale has been reported on the supplier’s 
BAS
–If yes, discuss the value of contacting a related/associated supplier with your team/audit leader, and what questions you need to include 
in contact
• Check whether the supplier has a Key Client Manager (KCM)If yes – contact the KCM to confirm if you are permitted to contact the
supplier.
Do not use contact details listed on invoices – source a phone number from independent sources.
This is because if a document has been altered or falsified, the contact details on the document may not belong to the actual supplier. 
INTERNAL
Third party checks
When you call the supplier:
• Identify yourself and ensure you are speaking with the person responsible for issuing invoices. For a larger business, it may be more 
appropriate to ask to speak to the accounts receivable team
• Provide ONLY the invoice number and date. Providing any other information may be a breach of privacy
• Do not name the client or the fact they are under audit.
Details we may need to confirm:
INTERNAL
Third party checks (continued)
GST audits: Fuel Tax Credit audits:
• Recipient’s name and address
• Total amount and amount of GST
• Goods or services supplied
• Date of supply
• Whether the tax invoice has been paid in full (if recipient is accounting on a 
cash basis) or if the tax invoice is more than 12 months’ old 
• Recipient’s name and address (if recorded)
• Total litres of each produce
• Date of purchase
If the third party check verifies the tax invoice provided, you can allow the GST claim as long as there’s creditable purpose.
If information provided by the third party is different to the invoice:
• Consider requesting a copy of the invoice from the supplier, then seek an explanation from the client regarding the differences
• If the supplier states they did not issue the invoice, note the risk and discuss with your team/audit leader before further engagement 
with the client.
If you are unable to contact the supplier, consider:
• Contacting the supplier’s registered agent
• Whether information in ATO systems can be used to mitigate the tax invoice risk (e.g. whether it appears the sale was reported in the 
suppliers BAS; whether RP Data or Veda check can verify the purchase)
• Issuing a written request for the information using eLetters third party contact template
INTERNAL
Third party checks (continued)
INTERNAL
Invalid tax invoices
Where tax invoices supplied are not valid, considering exercising the Commissioner’s discretion to treat them as valid. Refer to guidance 
contained in PS LA 2004/11.
Examples of situations where discretion may be exercised:
• Where there is a creditable acquisition or decreasing adjustment requiring a new invoice that has not issued
• Where there is a dispute between the parties about the transaction and you have other evidence to verify the transaction (payment in a 
bank statement, data in ECP, etc).
Examples of situations where discretion is not appropriate:
• Where there is fraud or evasion
• It is less than 28 days since the recipient asked the supplier to provide a valid tax invoice or adjustment note
• The recipient is simply unwilling to keep records.
GST transaction lists break down the sales and purchases reported in the BAS. 
When you receive a transaction list, check:
• Do GST on sales match the amount reported at label 1A?
• Do GST on purchases match the amount reported at label 1B?
• Are purchases correctly classified as taxable supplies – e.g. are there any GST-free or input taxed purchases classified as taxable 
purchases?
• Are the purchases claimed consistent with the enterprise the client is operating?
• Has the client claimed GST credits on private or domestic purchases?
• If they report on a cash basis, you may see multiple payments for a large invoice in the transaction list, so look to see if you can see the 
same transaction multiple times to determine if each amount adds up to the total amount of the tax invoice
