ATO Technical decision making model
Good decision-making model
As previously discussed, our audits primarily concern two risks:
• Is the client conducting an enterprise?
• Is the refund claimed on the BAS correct?
Decisions you need to consider:
• Is the client compliant – should case be finalised as NFA?
• Is a BAS revision required?
• Has the necessary information been provided, or is more information required?
• Is the case still appropriate for resolution within BAS A&A?
• Is the client entitled to their ABN and/or GST registration?
• Should penalties be considered?
Your team leader, audit leaders and support staff can assist in decision making.
INTERNAL
Making a decision
Ben applied for an ABN and registered for GST on 15 March 2023 declaring he was operating an enterprise of recycling and rubbish
removal. He registered the business name ‘Your Waste, Ben’s Treasure” and lodged his first activity statement on 23 March 2023 for the 
month of March 2023 declaring the following amounts:
G1 $400
1A $400
1B $20,000
Ben is expecting to receive a refund of $19,600.
Ben has not worked since he lost his job in 2020 around the time COVID-19 lockdowns occurred. He has been receiving Centrelink benefits 
since then and has no intention of looking for full time work.
In his spare time he picks up scrap and others items from road-side collections, restores them and then on sells to scrap yards or the 
general public. He does not bother advertising and doesn’t have a website or use social media. He is happy just sourcing all the products 
himself as he enjoys restoring them and selling to those who are interested.
INTERNAL
Decision making – example 1
His grandfather lets him use his old ute for the rubbish collection on the condition that he eventually buys it for $15,000 once he becomes 
financial. Ben has guessed that he has probably spent about $1,000 on tools and materials and estimates that his labour is worth about 
$4,000 for the time he has spent sourcing and fixing things.
Ben has only been able to sell a few items, which is normally for cash. He estimates it was around $400 but is not sure as he does not bank 
it. He is just happy he has a few spare dollars to spend when he sells his items.
Case officer Nancy successfully contacts Ben who confirms he lodged the activity statement. When Nancy starts to explain that we were 
conducting an audit and retaining the refund, Ben interrupted her saying he couldn’t talk as he was working and to just send him the details 
of what is needed, and he will respond.
Whilst Nancy was speaking to Ben she noticed the distinctive sound of poker machines in the background and noted this in her call note.
INTERNAL
Decision making – example 1 (continued)
Nancy issued the Audit Notification Letter giving Ben 14 days to respond. The request for information included the standard blurb within our 
procedures. 
Nancy did not make any further attempts to contact Ben to talk about his enterprise or remind him his documents were due. She had done 
many cases like this and just knew he wouldn’t talk to her or respond anyway.
Ben did not respond to the Audit Notification letter.
Nancy determined a no enterprise decision and issued the Finalisation letter. She did not attempt to call Ben to communicate the outcome.
