Decision making – example 2
On 15 April 2023 he lodged his first activity statement for the tax period 1 January 2023 to 31 March 2023 declaring the following amounts:
G1 $5,505
1A $500
1B $4,126
Hamish is expecting a refund of $3,626.
Case officer Robert successfully contacts Hamish who confirms he lodged the activity statement, and the amounts are correct. They 
continued to have an active conversation about Hamish’s new venture into Uber driving, how the activity statement was prepared and what 
the expenses relate too and how he funded the new car.
As Hamish did not have access to the tax invoice for the car at the time, Robert advised that his refund would be retained until we could 
confirm further information. Hamish agreed to provide this information and understood that he would be receiving an Audit Notification 
letter via email with instructions.
INTERNAL
Decision making – example 2 (continued)
Robert issued the Audit Notification requesting the following information for the period 1 January 2023 to 31 March 2023:
• A detailed transaction list showing business expenses including GST amounts
• Tax invoice for the Lexus
• Registration paperwork for Lexus showing owner and usage type
• Log book or similar showing business vs private use of Lexus
• Copies of bank statements showing payment of business expenses during the period
• Income or tax statements for each ride-source platform including Uber.
Hamish provided all of the requested information via email the next day.
Robert proceeded to review and analyse the information received. Upon carrying out the initial verification check on the tax invoice for the 
Lexus he discovered that the seller cancelled his GST registration on 15 June 2022. The seller was not registered for GST at the time he 
sold the car to Hamish.
All the other information Hamish provided was verified as correct.
Robert determined his decision, disallowing the GST claim for the purchase of the Lexus.
