What is a note?
• Is an electronic record
• Details interactions between a representative of the ATO and a taxpayer
• Recorded for every action you take on your case, including your reasoning for doing so
The ATO is committed to best practice in records management to ensure that: 
• It’s the digital record of authority for the ATO
• Records management is integrated into business systems and business processes
• ATO records are managed in the corporate domain 
• full and accurate records of all ATO activities and decisions are created, managed and retained or disposed of appropriately,
and in accordance with relevant legislation
• Records provide proof of what happened when it happened and who made the decisions. Not all records are of equal 
importance or need to be kept
• Records are kept for as long as required and are disposed of in accordance with the ATO’s disposal process.
INTERNAL
What do we include in our notes?
Review dates
Due dates for taxpayer’s response to provide information
The relevant warnings that have been provided
Clearly state the reasons for decisions
Details of actions taken or proposed to be taken
A concise record of relevant discussions
Proof of Record Ownership (PORO), Privacy and email disclosure procedures are followed and appropriately recorded
Full name and title/relationship to the entity of the person the case officer spoke with 
INTERNAL
What to exclude 
• emotive statements
• opinions or assumptions
• character judgments
• personal comments
• incorrect terms
• undefined time periods
• incomprehensible abbreviations
• uninformative comments
• inappropriate or obscene language
• irrelevant information - including unrelated or third-party information.
INTERNAL
When do we create a note
Notes must be added every time a case officer has an interaction with a taxpayer, takes action or makes a decision in 
relation to that taxpayer: 
• profiling
• record all contacts and attempted contact 
• actions and decisions that occur as a result of these discussions
• retention of refund decisions
• recording correspondence sent or received
• any delays for any reason (e.g. due to escalation or system issues) should be recorded including the expected timeframe 
• indirect actions that do not directly include the taxpayer must also be recorded, for example, where a case is transferred 
to another officer for a length of time
• mistakes - ATO or taxpayer
• internal meetings about the taxpayer
• decisions and recommendations for future action
INTERNAL
Notes and active case management
A case should not contain gaps that reflect periods where no apparent action has been taken. 
For a case to be actively managed an appropriate explanation for the gap is warranted. 
In certain circumstances, additional information must be recorded that relates to extenuating circumstances such as a death, 
illness or other significant incidents, fires, floods, that could justify delays.
INTERNAL
Writing clear notes
Writing a note is no different to writing anything else within the ATO. When writing a note you should:
• consider your audience and write for their needs
• follow plain English principles
• minimise the use of acronyms, abbreviations and jargon
• structure your writing to include all relevant information.
Keeping in mind the five C’s principles
❖ CLEAR
❖ CONCISE
❖ CORRECT
❖ COMPLETE
❖ COURTEOUS
INTERNAL
CLEAR - Clarity of a note
• notes should record what has been said 
• each sentence should contain only one complete thought
• your note should reflect and explain what you observed and what actions or decisions you took
• your note should be able to stand alone and be logical and understandable to a third person
• avoid jargon unless you are writing for specialists who understand it
• your spelling, grammar, capitalisation and punctuation should be correct
INTERNAL
CONCISE
• Your note should contain only the essential information, it does not need to record every action in minute detail.
• The aim is to ensure that your note and the issues are easily identified and understood.
• Use one word rather than a phrase where possible.
• Technical words, complex clauses and unnecessary wording should be avoided unless essential for clarity.
• Recording too much information is preferable to too little
INTERNAL
CORRECT
• It is important that your notes correctly record information such as dates, times, names and details of the conversation or 
event along with any documents or evidence that have been provided.
• Only record relevant information, irrelevant information (such as unrelated or third-party information) should not be 
recorded.
• Gather your information before you start writing.
• Facts should be correct.
• Statements should be free from distortion and ambiguity
• Written notes can be disposed of once they have transferred onto Siebel
• It is best to use the technique of he said/she said, e.g. ‘The client stated…’ followed by ‘I advised…’. 
• This is a preferred method because it correctly identifies who said what. 
• Attempting to summarise events can create bias and also result in important information being left out.
INTERNAL
CORRECT - timeliness
• Ensure all notes prepared after the interview or event are written within a reasonable timeframe
• They should be recorded as close to the event as possible
• The events should still be fresh in your memory at the time of any extra notes being written (preferably on the same day)
• This is important as there have been court cases lost due to untimely notes being considered unreliable by the court 
(inadmissible evidence)
INTERNAL
COMPLETE
• Your note should be complete in all the necessary details so that clarification with the author (you) isn’t needed
• Your note should answer all of the questions raised
• After you have written your note look over it again and edit it and check to ensure that all material facts have been recorded
INTERNAL
COURTEOUS
• Use the active voice rather than the passive voice
• Use a neutral tone as it conveys a sense of courtesy and cooperation and is not emotive or discriminatory.
• Use the word ‘you’ as it establishes a rapport with the reader.
• Use the word ‘we’ when referring to ATO staff.
• Eliminate sexist language - gender should be avoided unless it is relevant to the discussion.
• Your note should reflect a polite and courteous tone and you should consider the effect it will have on the reader.
INTERNAL
Knowing your audience
The first key principle to writing clear notes
• Does this note meet the needs of likely readers?
• If I was in the readers shoes, would this note pass the pub test and deliver what I need it to
• Do I have a primary or immediate audience as opposed to a secondary or downstream audience
• What's the aim of my case? Is that clearly articulated for context and do my notes support this aim and my own decision 
making process?
INTERNAL
Acronyms and abbreviations
• We use acronyms, initialisms, abbreviations and jargon terms every day in the ATO. 
• It is this commonplace familiarity that means we use them in our written work quite naturally and often without consciously 
realising we have done so. 
• Yet what makes sense to you may make no sense to someone else.
Communication – ATO style guide.pdf | - All Documents
INTERNAL 
Acronym/initialism
• Acronyms and initialisms refer to a set of initial letters that act in place of the full name of something. 
• Although both are commonly called 'acronyms', technically most are 'initialisms’. 
• In an acronym, the initials can be pronounced and used as a word (QANTAS, SCUBA and LASER for example). 
• Initialisms (USB, ATO, GST or FBT, for example) are more common.
• If you need to use any acronyms or abbreviations, state the full word or words the first time, with the shortened version in 
brackets. You can then use the shortened version in the remainder of the note.
Acronyms and initialisms | Style Manual
INTERNAL
Jargon
• Jargon refers to special words or expressions used by a profession or group that may be different, or difficult for others to
understand. For example, the alternative meanings of 'router', depending on whether you are talking about IT or 
woodworking.
• It also includes words that seem ordinary to a non-specialist, but which have special meanings. For example, the legal 
meaning of common words such as: 'gain', 'private', 'ordinary meaning' or 'material difference'.
