What is third party fraud?
In a nutshell, third party fraud occurs when a client’s identity, personal details, or accounts have been used without authority. This access is then 
been used to generate a benefit to a third party (someone who is not the client) through lodge false BAS or other reporting to generate a refund or 
reversing a genuine report to credit back legitimate payments.
This can take multiple forms:
• A person loses control of their identity documents and an unknown person has sufficient information about an individual to create a fake myGov link
– They register them as a sole trader by registering them for an Australian Business Number (ABN) and GST
– Lodge Business Activity Statements (BAS) for large refunds
– Add their bank account
– All actions have been done without the knowledge or authority of the individual who actually not in business
• A genuine registered business entity with a history of reporting payable BAS has found the following:
– This entity had changes to addresses and bank accounts
– Amendments to payable BAS or new BAS lodged with large refunds
– The lodgements do not match prior reporting patterns
– This can also include “new” tax agents being registered to lodge
• A authorised person has had their credentials stolen and used for the purposes of lodging fraud – this can include authorised contacts or Tax/BAS 
agents
• This is just a sample of third party fraud and this list is not exhaustive
INTERNAL 2
OFFICIAL
OFFICIAL
Authority to act
If a client “authorises” someone to access or act on their behalf then this is 
not considered third party fraud. This can sometimes include a Tax or BAS 
agent authorisation (we will go through this later)
Joe and his mate, Bob, are catching up talking about their future. Joe has 
plans to start a business and be his own boss. 
His mate Bob says “I can help you get a loan to start your business. It’s 
easy the government is giving loans”. 
Joe asks him “how?”
Bob says “you can do it through your myGov account, let me show you”
Joe hands over his phone and gives Bob his password to access his 
myGov account. Bob then sets up an ABN, registers Joe for GST and 
lodges a BAS to claim $30K refund which will be paid to Bob’s bank 
account. 
Bob tells Joe that “it’s ok, it’s a loan from the government”.
Although the actions were done by Bob and the refund will be paid to his 
account, would you consider this to be third party fraud?
