How GST works
Goods and services tax (GST) is a broad-based tax of 10% on most goods, services and other items sold or consumed in 
Australia.
Some things don’t have GST included, these are called GST-free sales.
Generally, businesses and other organisations registered for GST will:
• include GST in the price they charge for their goods and services
• claim credits for the GST included in the price of goods and services they buy for their business
INTERNAL
UNOFFICIAL
UNOFFICIAL
What a business needs to do for GST
If a business or other enterprise and has a GST turnover of $75,000 or more ($150,000 or more for non-profit organisations), or 
provide taxi travel (including ride-sourcing) – they will need to:
• Register for GST
• Work out whether sales are taxable (That is, subject to GST and not exempt because they are GST-free or input taxed and 
include GST in the price of the taxable sales)
• Issue tax invoices for taxable sales and obtain tax invoices for business purchases
• Claim GST credits for GST included in the price of their business purchases
• Account for GST on either a cash or non-cash basis and put aside the GST collected so they can pay it when it’s due
• Lodge activity statements or annual returns to report sales and purchases, and pay GST or receive a GST refund.
